indication:
&lt;p&gt;This is a limited coverage policy for assays that detect biomarkers from circulating tumor cells (CTCs).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Criteria for Coverage&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;Cancers with established biomarker testing, as recommended by society or national guidelines&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Assays that detect biomarkers from CTCs are covered when ALL of the following are met:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The patient has been diagnosed with cancer&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The specific cancer type has an associated biomarker&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The associated biomarker has already established clinical utility (CU) in the peer-reviewed published literature for the intended cancer type and for the specific indication in the intended patient population
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The biomarker&amp;#8217;s CU may include any of the following: it can be used to diagnose, risk-stratify, predict, or monitor response to therapy, as recommended by national or society guidelines (i.e., American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO), National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN))&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;At least 1 of the following criteria are met AND there is clear documentation of at least 1 of these in the medical record:
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The patient&amp;#8217;s cancer has not previously been tested for the specific biomarker, &lt;strong&gt;OR&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The patient has newly metastatic cancer, and a metastatic lesion has not been tested for the specific biomarker, &lt;strong&gt;OR&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The patient demonstrates signs of clinical, radiological or pathologic disease progression, &lt;strong&gt;OR&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;There is concern for resistance to treatment based on specific and well-established clinical indications&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testing for the biomarker can be performed using CTCs&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The CTC-based biomarker test successfully completes a comprehensive Technical Assessment (TA) by Molecular Diagnostic Services Program (MolDX&lt;sup&gt;&amp;#174;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;) that will ensure that Analytical Validity (AV) (including analytical and clinical validation), Clinical Validity (CV), and CU criteria are met to establish the test as Reasonable and Necessary.
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The clinical validation has demonstrated performance that is equivalent or superior to tissue-based testing or another already-accepted test for the same biomarker for the same intended use.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CV (for new analytes) must be established through studies published in the peer-reviewed literature for the intended use of the test in the intended population.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tissue-based testing for the specific biomarker is infeasible (e.g., quantity not sufficient or invasive biopsy is medically contraindicated) &lt;strong&gt;OR&lt;&sol;strong&gt; will not provide sufficient information for subsequent medical management (e.g., in cases where human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2) overexpression is negative in a tissue biopsy but may be positive in the CTCs, due to tumor heterogeneity). There is clear documentation of at least 1 of these reasons for testing in the medical record.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For a given patient encounter, only 1 test for assessing the biomarker may be performed UNLESS a second test, meeting all the criteria established herein, is reasonable and necessary as an adjunct to the first test.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Duplicate testing of the same biomarker (&lt;em&gt;from the same sample type and for the same clinical indication&lt;&sol;em&gt;) using different methodologies is not covered. For example, testing for androgen receptor splice variant 7 (AR-V7) from CTCs by messenger RNA (mRNA) as well as immunohistochemistry (IHC)-based methodologies, for the same clinical indication, will not be covered.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;

