indication:
&lt;p&gt;Blepharoplasty, blepharoptosis and lid reconstruction may be defined as any eyelid surgery that improves abnormal function, reconstructs deformities, or enhances appearance. They may be either functional&sol;reconstructive or cosmetic. Upper blepharoplasty (removal of upper eyelid skin) and&sol;or repair of blepharoptosis should be considered functional&sol;reconstructive in nature when the upper lid position or overhanging skin or brow is sufficiently low to produce functional complaints, usually related to visual field impairment whether in primary gaze or down-gaze reading position. Upper blepharoplasty may also be indicated for chronic dermatitis due to redundant skin. Another indication for blepharoptosis surgery is patients with an anophthalmic socket experiencing ptosis or prosthesis difficulties. Brow ptosis (i.e., descent or droop of the eyebrows) can also produce or contribute to functional impairment.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The criteria in section A (patient signs and symptoms), and section B (visual field) below must be documented to demonstrate medical necessity.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A. Documentation in the medical records must include patient complaints and findings secondary to eyelid or brow malposition such as:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Interference with vision or visual field, related to activities such as, difficulty reading due to upper eyelid drooping, looking through the eyelashes, seeing the upper eyelid skin, or brow fatigue.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Chronic eyelid dermatitis due to redundant skin.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Difficulty wearing prosthesis, artificial eye.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Margin reflex distance (MRD) of 2.5 mm or less.&lt;br &sol;&gt;(The margin reflex distance is a measurement from the corneal light reflex to the upper eyelid margin with the brows relaxed.)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;A palpebral fissure height on down-gaze of 1 mm or less.&lt;br &sol;&gt;(The down-gaze palpebral fissure height is measured with the patient fixating on an object in down-gaze with the ipsilateral brow relaxed and the contralateral lid elevated.)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;The presence of Herring&amp;#8217;s effect meeting 1 of the above 2 (#4 or 5) criteria.&lt;br &sol;&gt;(Herring&amp;#8217;s law is 1 of equal innervation to both upper eyelids and is considered in the documentation to perform bilateral ptosis in which the position of 1 upper eyelid has marginal criteria, and the other eyelid has good supportive documentation for ptosis surgery. In these cases, the surgeon can lift the more ptotic lid with tape or instillation of Phenylephrine drops into the superior fornix. If the less ptotic lid then drops downward according to Herring&amp;#8217;s law to the point of an MRD of 2.5 mm or less or a down-gaze MRD of 1.5 or less or a palpebral fissure width on down-gaze of 1 mm or less, then the less ptotic lid would be considered for surgical correction.)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;B. Visual fields&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;The indication for surgery is supported if a difference of 12&amp;#186; or more or 30% superior visual field difference is demonstrated between visual field testing before and after manual elevation of the eyelids.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Visually significant brow ptosis may be documented by visual field testing with the brow elevated demonstrating a difference of 12&amp;#186; or more or 30% superior visual field difference.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Visual fields need to meet accepted quality standards, whether they are performed by the &lt;br &sol;&gt;Goldmann perimeter technique or by use of a standardized automated perimetry technique.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;Visual fields are not necessary for patients with an anophtholmic socket who is experiencing ptosis or difficulty with their prosthesis.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;C. Relief of eye symptoms associated with blepharospasm. Primary essential idiopathic blepharospasm is characterized by severe squinting, secondary to uncontrollable spasms of the periorbital muscles. Occasionally, it can be debilitating. If other treatments have failed or are contraindicated (i.e., an injection of Botulinum Toxin A,) an extended blepharoplasty with wide resection of the orbicularis oculi muscle complex may be necessary. (See Botulinum Toxin Type A and Type B, L34635)&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;#167;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;42 CFR &amp;#167;410.32 Diagnostic x-ray tests, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CMS Internet Online Manual Pub. 100-02 Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, Chapter 15, Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, &amp;#167;80.1.1 Certification Changes.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, Section 1862(a)(1)(A) states that no Medicare payment shall be made for items or services which are not reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, Section 1862(a)(7). This section excludes routine physical examinations.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, Section 1833(e) states that no payment shall be made to any provider for any claim that lacks the necessary information to process the claim.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;Intraoperative neurophysiological testing may be used to identify&sol;prevent complications during surgery on the nervous system, its blood supply, or adjacent tissue.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Monitoring can identify new neurologic impairment, identify, or separate nervous system structures (e.g., around or in a tumor), and can demonstrate which tracts or nerves are still functional. Intraoperative neurophysiological testing may provide relative reassurance to the surgeon that no identifiable complication has been detected up to a certain point, allowing the surgeon to proceed further and provide a more thorough or careful surgical intervention than would have been provided in the absence of monitoring.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Some high-risk patients may be candidates for a surgical procedure only if monitoring is available. To establish medical necessity the following guidelines must be followed:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;Intraoperative testing may be indicated with the following types of surgery:&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Surgery of the aortic arch, its branch vessels, or thoracic aorta, including internal carotid artery surgery, when there is risk of cerebral ischemia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resection of epileptogenic brain tissue or tumor&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resection of brain tissue close to the primary motor cortex and requiring brain mapping&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protection of cranial nerves:&lt;br &sol;&gt;
&lt;ol type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;tumors that are optic, trigeminal, facial, auditory nerves&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cavernous sinus tumors&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;oval or round window graft&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;endolymphatic shunt for M&amp;#233;ni&amp;#232;re&#39;s disease&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;vestibular section for vertigo&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;microvascular decompression of cranial nerves&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Correction of scoliosis or deformity of spinal cord involving traction on the cord&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protection of spinal cord where work is performed in close proximity to cord as in the removal of old hardware or where there have been numerous interventions&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Spinal instrumentation requiring pedicle screws or distraction&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decompressive procedures on the spinal cord or cauda equina carried out for myelopathy or claudication where function of spinal cord or spinal nerves is at risk&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resection of:&lt;br &sol;&gt;
&lt;ol type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Spinal cord tumors&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Neuromas of peripheral nerves or brachial plexus, when there is risk to major sensory or motor nerves&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Surgery for:&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;ol type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;intracranial AV malformations&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;arteriovenous malformation of spinal cord&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;surgery for intractable movement disorders&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cerebral vascular aneurysms&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Arteriography, during which there is a test occlusion of the carotid artery&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Circulatory arrest with hypothermia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Distal aortic procedures, where there is risk of ischemia to spinal cord&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Leg lengthening procedures, where there is traction on sciatic nerve or other nerve trunks&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Basil ganglia movement disorders&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Surgery as a result of traumatic injury to spinal cord&sol;brain&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deep brain stimulation&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;For reimbursement, this test must be requested by the operating surgeon and the monitoring must be performed by a physician, other than:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;The operating surgeon&lt;br &sol;&gt;The technical&sol;surgical assistant; or&lt;br &sol;&gt;The anesthesiologist rendering the anesthesia&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The benefits of intraoperative neurophysiologic testing are attainable under optimal recording and interpreting conditions.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Due to the nature of these services and the potential for significant morbidity in some procedures requiring intraoperative monitoring, Medicare expects to see these services used in the hospital setting only. As the level of anesthesia may significantly impact the ability to interpret intraoperative studies, continuous communication between the anesthesiologist and the monitoring physician is expected when medically indicated.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is also expected that a specifically trained technician, preferably registered with one of the credentialing organizations, will be in continuous attendance in the operating room, recording and monitoring a single surgical case, with either the physical or electronic capacity for real-time communication with the supervising neurologist or other physician trained in neurophysiology.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Intraoperative monitoring is not medically necessary in situations where historical data and current practices reveal no potential for damage to neural integrity during surgery. Monitoring under these circumstances will exceed the patient&#39;s medical need.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Due to the potential risk for morbidity with many of the above noted surgeries and the need for explicit and focused attention to both the monitoring and the procedure, Medicare does not expect to see operating surgeons submitting claims for this code. Monitoring may be performed from a remote site, as long as a trained technician (see detail above) will be in continuous attendance in the operating room, with either the physical or electronic capacity for real-time communication with the supervising physician (MD&sol;DO). Technical criteria (mandatory) include that at least 8 recording channels be available (16 if EEG is monitored) for all intraoperative neurophysiological monitoring. The remotely supervising physician must have the ability to watch the tracings as they are obtained in real-time in the operating room, as well as the baseline electrophysiological test and the monitoring tracings from earlier in the case.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Technical criteria (mandatory) for remote monitoring also include (a) routine real-time auditory or written communication between the supervising physician and the operating room and (b) the capability for telephone communications as needed between the supervising physician and the monitoring technologist, operating surgeon, and the anesthesiologist.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The equipment must also provide for all of the monitoring modalities that may be applied - auditory-evoked response, electroencephalography&sol;electrocorticography, electromyography and nerve conduction, and somatosensory-evoked response.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Undivided attention to a unique patient may be required during some surgeries, such as during response to acute events or identification of the cerebral cortex to be resected or spared from resection. The monitoring physician must have a plan in place to transfer care to another physician of any other case during those times. When paying undivided attention to a unique patient, the physician must code and bill only for that one case during those times. For other medically necessary intraoperative neurophysiologic monitoring, a physician may monitor up to 3 cases simultaneously.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Medicare does not provide for reimbursement of &quot;incident to&quot; care in the hospital setting. More than one patient may be monitored at once; however, claims for physician services must be submitted only for the time devoted to monitoring when attention is directed exclusively to one patient.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(7) states Medicare will not cover any services or procedures associated with routine physical checkups.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;42 CFR410.32(a) requires a clinical diagnostic test be ordered by the physician who is treating the patient for a specific medical problem and uses the results in the management of the beneficiary&amp;rsquo;s specific problem.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This Local Coverage Determination (LCD) identifies the indications and limitations of Medicare coverage and reimbursement for Vitamin D laboratory assays in the medical management of patients.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For Medicare beneficiaries, screening tests are governed by statute. Reimbursement is not allowed for routine screening for vitamin D deficiency in asymptomatic individuals and&sol;or during general encounters.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Although it is not the active form of the hormone, 25-OH vitamin D is much more commonly measured because it better reflects the sum total of vitamin D produced endogenously and absorbed from the diet than does the level of the active hormone 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin. 25-hydroxyvitamin D [25(OH)D] testing will be considered medically reasonable and necessary for individuals at risk for deficiency with the following conditions:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Chronic kidney disease (CKD) stage III or greater&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Cystic Fibrosis (CF)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Cirrhosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Crohn&amp;rsquo;s disease&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Gastric bypass&sol;bariatric surgery&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Granuloma forming diseases&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Hyperalimentation&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Hypocalcemia&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Hypercalcemia&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Hypercalciuria&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Hypervitaminosis D&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Hypovitaminosis D&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Inflammatory bowel disease&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Long term use of medications known to lower vitamin d levels: anticonvulsants, antiretroviral therapy, glucocorticoids, antifungals, and cholestyramine&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Parathyroid disorders&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Paget&amp;rsquo;s disease of bone&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Obesity, if Body Mass Index (BMI) &amp;ge; 30 kg&sol;m2&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Obstructive jaundice&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Osteomalacia&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Osteopenia&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Osteoporosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Osteosclerosis&sol;petrosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Pregnant and lactating women&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Radiation enteritis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Rickets&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Vitamin D deficiency on replacement therapy related to a condition listed above, to monitor the efficacy of treatment&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Deficiency of 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D, which is present at much lower concentrations, does not necessarily reflect deficiency of 25-OH vitamin D and its measurement should be limited to specific clinical situations. It will be considered reasonable and necessary for patients with the following conditions:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Unexplained hypercalcemia &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Unexplained hypercalciuria&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Suspected genetic childhood rickets&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Suspected tumor induced osteomalacia&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Nephrolithiasis &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Renal osteodystrophy&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Sarcoidosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Once a beneficiary has been shown to be vitamin D deficient with a serum level of &amp;lt;30 ng&sol;ml, a repeat test after 12 weeks of supplementation will be considered reasonable and necessary to ensure adequate replacement has been accomplished. The medical record must reflect that the beneficiary has been compliant with supplementation. A serum level of &amp;ge;30 ng&sol;ml will be considered evidence of adequate replacement, and no further testing is necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;If, after a 12-week period of supplementation and documentation of compliance with the prescribed supplementation, the serum level is still &amp;lt;30 ng&sol;ml 1 additional repeat testing within a rolling 12-month period of the initial test, may be performed.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Thereafter, annual testing may be appropriate depending upon the indication and other mitigating factors. The documentation must support the need for annual testing. Annual testing should be rare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

