&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act Section 1833(e) prohibits Medicare payment for any claim which lacks the necessary information to process the claim.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act Section 1862(a)(1)(A) excludes expenses incurred for items or services which are not reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Code of Federal Regulations 42 CFR Section 410.32 indicates that diagnostic tests may only be ordered by the treating physician or other treating practitioner acting within the scope of his or her license and Medicare requirements who furnishes a consultation or treats a beneficiary for a specific medical problem and who uses the results in the management of the beneficiary&#39;s specific medical problem. Tests not ordered by the physician (or another qualified non-physician provider) who is treating the beneficiary are not reasonable and necessary (see Sec. 411.15(k)(1) of this chapter).&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;VEP&sol;VER&lt;&sol;strong&gt; - The visual evoked response (VER) and visual evoked potential (VEP) evaluate the visual nervous system pathways from the eyes to the occipital cortex of the brain. By measuring the function of the entire visual pathway, it helps to separate eye disease from central nervous system defects. VER&sol;VEP involves stimulation of the retina and optic nerve with a shifting checkerboard pattern or flash method. This external visual stimulus causes measurable electrical activity in neurons within the visual pathways. The VER is recorded by electroencephalography electrodes located over the occiput, producing a characteristic waveform. Abnormalities may be seen in a variety of pathologic processes involving the optic nerve and its radiations. Pattern-shift VER is a highly sensitive means of documenting lesions in the visual system.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ERG&lt;&sol;strong&gt; - The full field electroretinogram (ERG) is used to detect loss of retinal function or distinguish between retinal and optic nerve lesions. ERG measures the electrical activity generated by neural and non-neuronal cells in the retina in response to a light stimulus. ERGs are usually obtained using electrodes embedded in a corneal contact lens, or a thin wire inside the lower eyelid, which measure a summation of retinal electrical activity at the corneal surface. The International Society for Clinical Electrophysiology of Vision (ISCEV) introduced minimum standards for the ERG in 1989. The ERG helps to distinguish retinal degeneration and dystrophies. Multi-focal electroretinography (mfERG) is a higher resolution form of ERG, enabling assessment of ERG activity in small areas of the retina. Pattern ERG (PERG) to assess retinal ganglion cell (RGC) function in glaucoma is being investigated.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Indications of Coverage&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Visual Evoked Potentials or Responses (VEPs&sol;VERs)&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm diagnosis of multiple sclerosis when clinical criteria are inconclusive&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluate diseases of the optic nerve, such as:&lt;br &sol;&gt;
&lt;ol type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optic neuritis&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ischemic optic neuropathy&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Toxic amblyopias&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Nutritional amblyopias&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Neoplasms compressing the anterior visual pathways&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optic nerve injury or atrophy&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Malingering&sol;functional vision loss (to rule out)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Monitor the visual system during optic nerve (or related) surgery (monitoring of short-latency evoked potential studies)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Electroretinography (ERG)&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To diagnose loss of retinal function or distinguish between retinal lesions and optic nerve lesions: &lt;br &sol;&gt;
&lt;ol type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Toxic retinopathies, including those caused by intraocular metallic foreign bodies and Vigabatrin&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Diabetic retinopathy&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ischemic retinopathies including central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO), branch vein occlusion (BVO), and sickle cell retinopathy&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Autoimmune retinopathies such as Cancer Associated Retinopathy (CAR), Melanoma Associated Retinopathy (MAR), and Acute Zonal Occult Outer Retinopathy (AZOOR)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retinal detachment&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Assessment of retinal function after trauma, especially in vitreous hemorrhage, dense cataracts, and other conditions where the fundus cannot be visualized photoreceptors; absent b-wave indicates abnormality in the bipolar cell region.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retinitis pigmentosa and related hereditary degenerations&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retinitis punctata albescens&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Leber&#39;s congenital amaurosis&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Choroideremia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gyrate atrophy of the retina and choroid&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Goldmann-Favre syndrome&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Congenital stationary night blindness&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;X-linked juvenile retinoschisis&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Achromatopsia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cone dystrophy&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Disorders mimicking retinitis pigmentosa&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Usher Syndrome&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To detect chloroquine (Aralen) and hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) toxicity (mfERG) per the American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO) guidelines (10).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;VEP&sol;ERG in Glaucoma (Non-Covered)&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;A 2011 report by the AAO on &amp;ldquo;Assessment of Visual Function in Glaucoma&amp;rdquo; noted that while VEP and ERG, as objective measures of visual function, provided testing free of patient input, issues prevent their adoption for glaucoma management. It concluded that advances in technology have yet to produce definitive guidance on the diagnosis of glaucoma or its progression over time and that further research on an objective measure of visual function is needed.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since then, several studies have investigated the use of VEP and ERG technology to differentiate between normal healthy eyes and eyes with early to advanced visual field loss resulting from glaucoma. The authors indicated that VEP and ERG may allow earlier diagnosis of glaucoma. However, without larger studies, WPS GHA and the AAO&amp;rsquo;s 2011 report both agree that these technologies have yet to produce definitive guidance on the diagnosis of glaucoma or its progression over time. This was also the conclusion of a 2013 study which prospectively monitored progressive changes of RGC function in early glaucoma using PERG. The authors concluded that further follow-up is required to determine whether PERG losses are predictors of future visual field loss.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Neither of the 2015 AAO Preferred Practice Guidelines, &amp;ldquo;Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma Suspect&amp;rdquo; or &amp;ldquo;Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma,&amp;rdquo; mention VEP or ERG as diagnostic tools. Also, the UpToDate&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; review on &amp;ldquo;Open-angle glaucoma: Epidemiology, clinical presentation, and diagnosis,&amp;rdquo; likewise omits reference to either test.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There remain no verified guidelines for normal vs abnormal that would be easily applicable to an individual patient. WPS GHA, therefore, considers the use of VEP or ERG for either glaucoma diagnosis or management investigational.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Testing shall be performed by physicians who have evidence of knowledge, training, and expertise to perform and interpret these tests. This training and expertise must have been acquired within the framework of an accredited school, residency, or fellowship program.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

