&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Other Covered Services&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reflex Testing by Reference Laboratories &amp;ndash; since reference laboratories do not have access to patient-specific data, reflex testing under the following circumstances is reasonable and necessary:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To verify a presumptive positive UDT using definitive methods that include but are not limited to GC-MS or LC-MS&sol;MS before reporting the presumptive finding to the ordering clinician and without an additional order from the clinician; or&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To confirm the absence of prescribed medications when a negative result is obtained by presumptive UDT in the laboratory for a prescribed medication listed by the ordering clinician.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When medical record documentation that is individualized for a particular patient satisfies medical necessity requirements found elsewhere in this LCD (e.g., risk assessment, frequency), direct to definitive UDT without a presumptive UDT may be reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Definitive testing to confirm a negative presumptive UDT result, upon the order of the clinician, is reasonable and necessary in the following circumstances:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The result is inconsistent with a patient&amp;rsquo;s self-report, presentation, medical history, or current prescribed medication plan (should be present in the sample);&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Following a review of clinical findings, the clinician suspects use of a substance that is inadequately detected or not detected by a presumptive UDT; or&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When there is an unexpected negative presumptive UDT result, and it is clinically imperative to know if it is truly positive or negative; the medical record should state such.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Definitive testing to confirm a presumptive UDT positive result, upon the order of the clinician, is reasonable and necessary when the result is inconsistent with the expected result, a patient&amp;rsquo;s self-report, presentation, medical history, or current prescribed medication plan.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Non-Covered Services-therefore not reasonable and necessary services&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;div&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Blanket Orders-same orders for all patients in a health care provider&amp;rsquo;s practice.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reflex definitive UDT is not reasonable and necessary when presumptive testing is performed at point of care because the clinician may have sufficient information to manage the patient. If the clinician is not satisfied, he&sol;she must determine the clinical appropriateness of and order specific subsequent definitive testing (e.g., the patient admits to using a particular drug, or the IA cut-off is set at such a point that is sufficiently low that the physician is satisfied with the presumptive test result).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Routine standing orders for all patients in a physician&amp;rsquo;s practice are not reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is not reasonable and necessary for a physician to perform presumptive POCT and order presumptive IA testing from a reference laboratory. In other words, Medicare will only pay for one presumptive test result per patient per date of service regardless of the number of billing providers.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is not reasonable and necessary for a physician to perform presumptive IA testing and order presumptive IA testing from a reference laboratory. Medicare will only pay for one presumptive test result per patient per date of service regardless of the number of billing providers.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is not reasonable and necessary for a reference laboratory to perform and bill IA presumptive UDT prior to definitive testing without a specific physician&amp;rsquo;s order for the presumptive testing.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IA testing, regardless of whether it is qualitative or semi-quantitative (numerical), may not be used to &amp;ldquo;confirm&amp;rdquo; or definitively identify a presumptive test result obtained by cups, dipsticks, cards, cassettes, or other IA testing methods. Definitive UDT provides specific identification and&sol;or quantification typically by GC-MS or LC-MS&sol;MS. Semi-Quantitative is defined as a numerical estimation of the approximate concentrations.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drug testing of 2 different specimen types from the same patient on the same date of service for the same drugs&sol;metabolites&sol;analytes.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;UDT for medico-legal and&sol;or employment purposes or to protect a physician from drug diversion charges.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Specimen validity testing including, but not limited to, pH, specific gravity, oxidants, creatinine.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;

