&lt;h3&gt;Other Covered Services&lt;&sol;h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reflex Testing by Reference Laboratories &amp;ndash; since reference laboratories do not have access to patient-specific data, reflex testing under the following circumstances is reasonable and necessary:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To verify a presumptive positive UDT using definitive methods that include but are not limited to GC-MS or LC-MS&sol;MS before reporting the presumptive finding to the ordering clinician and without an additional order from the clinician; or&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To confirm the absence of prescribed medications when a negative result is obtained by presumptive UDT in the laboratory for a prescribed medication listed by the ordering clinician.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When medical record documentation that is individualized for a particular patient satisfies medical necessity requirements found elsewhere in this LCD (e.g., risk assessment, frequency), direct to definitive UDT without a presumptive UDT may be reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Definitive testing to confirm a negative presumptive UDT result, upon the order of the clinician, is reasonable and necessary in the following circumstances:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The result is inconsistent with a patient&amp;rsquo;s self-report, presentation, medical history, or current prescribed medication plan (should be present in the sample);&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Following a review of clinical findings, the clinician suspects use of a substance that is inadequately detected or not detected by a presumptive UDT; or&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When there is an unexpected negative presumptive UDT result, and it is clinically imperative to know if it is truly positive or negative; the medical record should state such.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Definitive testing to confirm a presumptive UDT positive result, upon the order of the clinician, is reasonable and necessary when the result is inconsistent with the expected result, a patient&amp;rsquo;s self-report, presentation, medical history, or current prescribed medication plan.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Non-Covered Services&lt;&sol;h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Blanket Orders-same orders for all patients in a health care provider&#39;s practice.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reflex definitive UDT is not reasonable and necessary when presumptive testing is performed at point of care because the clinician may have sufficient information to manage the patient. If the clinician is not satisfied, he&sol;she must determine the clinical appropriateness of and order specific subsequent definitive testing (e.g., the patient admits to using a particular drug, or the IA cut-off is set at such a point that is sufficiently low that the physician is satisfied with the presumptive test result).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Routine standing orders for all patients in a physician&amp;rsquo;s practice are not reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is not reasonable and necessary for a physician to perform presumptive POCT and order presumptive IA testing from a reference laboratory. In other words, Medicare will only pay for one presumptive test result per patient per date of service regardless of the number of billing providers.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is not reasonable and necessary for a physician to perform presumptive IA testing and order presumptive IA testing from a reference laboratory. Medicare will only pay for one presumptive test result per patient per date of service regardless of the number of billing providers.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is not reasonable and necessary for a reference laboratory to perform and bill IA presumptive UDT prior to definitive testing without a specific physician&amp;rsquo;s order for the presumptive testing.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IA testing, regardless of whether it is qualitative or semi-quantitative (numerical), may not be used to &amp;ldquo;confirm&amp;rdquo; or definitively identify a presumptive test result obtained by cups, dipsticks, cards, cassettes or other IA testing methods. Definitive UDT provides specific identification and&sol;or quantification typically by GC-MS or LC-MS&sol;MS. Semi-Quantitative is defined as a numerical estimation of the approximate concentrations.&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drug testing of 2 different specimen types from the same patient on the same date of service for the same drugs&sol;metabolites&sol;analytes.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;UDT for medico-legal and&sol;or employment purposes or to protect a physician from drug diversion charges.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Specimen validity testing including, but not limited to, pH, specific gravity, oxidants, creatinine.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vitamin D is called a &quot;vitamin&quot; because of its exogenous source, predominately from oily fish in the form of vitamin D &lt;sub&gt;2&lt;&sol;sub&gt; and vitamin D&lt;sub&gt; 3&lt;&sol;sub&gt;. It is more accurate to consider fat-soluble Vitamin D as a steroid hormone, synthesized by the skin and metabolized by the kidney to an active hormone, calcitriol. Clinical disorders related to vitamin D may arise because of altered availability of the parent vitamin D, altered conversion of vitamin D to its predominant metabolites, altered organ responsiveness to dihydroxylated metabolites and disturbances in the interactions of the vitamin D metabolites with PTH and calcitonin. Normal levels of Vitamin D range from 20 &amp;ndash; 50 ng&sol;ml. This LCD identifies the indications and limitations of Medicare coverage and reimbursement for the lab assay.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Indications: &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Measurement of 25-OH Vitamin D level is indicated for patients with:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;chronic kidney disease stage III or greater&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cirrhosis&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;hypocalcemia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;hypercalcemia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;hypercalciuria&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;hypervitaminosis D&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;parathyroid disorders&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;malabsorption states&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;obstructive jaundice&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;osteomalacia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;osteoporosis if&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;i. T score on DEXA scan &amp;lt;-2.5 or&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;ii. History of fragility fractures or&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;iii. FRAX &amp;gt; 3% 10-year probability of hip fracture or 20% 10-year probability of other major osteoporotic fracture or&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;iv. FRAX &amp;gt; 3% (any fracture) with T-score &amp;lt;-1.5 or&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;v. Initiating bisphosphanate therapy (Vitamin D level should be determined and managed as necessary&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;before bisphosphonate is initiated)&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;osteosclerosis&sol;petrosis&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;rickets&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;vitamin D deficiency on replacement therapy related to a condition listed above; to monitor the efficacy of treatment.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Measurement of 1, 25-OH Vitamin D level is indicated for patients with:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;unexplained hypercalcemia (suspected granulomatous disease or lymphoma)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;unexplained hypercalciuria (suspected granulomatous disease or lymphoma)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;suspected genetic childhood rickets&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;suspected tumor-induced osteomalacia&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;nephrolithiasis or hypercalciuria&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Limitations: &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Testing may not be used for routine or other screening.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Both assays of vitamin D need not be performed for each of the above conditions. Often, one type is more appropriate for a certain disease state than another. The most common type of vitamin D deficiency is 25-OH vitamin D. A much smaller percentage of 1, 25-dihydroxy vitamin D deficiency exists; mostly, in those with renal disease. Although it is not the active form of the hormone, 25-OH vitamin D is much more commonly measured because it better reflects the sum total of vitamin D produced endogenously and absorbed from the diet than does the level of the active hormone 1, 25-dihydroxy vitamin D. Deficiency of 1, 25-dihydroxy vitamin D, which is present at much lower concentrations, does not necessarily reflect deficiency of 25-OH vitamin D and its measurement should be limited to the indications listed. Documentation must justify the test(s) chosen for a particular disease entity. Various component sources of 25-OH vitamin D, such as stored D or diet-derived D, should not be billed separately.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Once a beneficiary has been shown to be vitamin D deficient, further testing may be medically necessary only to ensure adequate replacement has been accomplished. If Vitamin D level is between 20 and 50 ng&sol;ml and patient is clinically stable, repeat testing is often unnecessary; if performed, documentation most clearly indicate the necessity of the test. If level &amp;lt;20 ng&sol;ml or &amp;gt; 60 ng&sol;ml, a subsequent level(s) may be reimbursed until the level is within the normal range.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

