&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Coronary Computed Tomography Angiography (CCTA)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; - A&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;non-invasive test using advanced computed tomography angiography imaging to view the tissues and blood vessels of the heart. This can be used to determine the presence and extent of CAD.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Coronary Plaque Analysis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt; (CPA)&lt;&sol;em&gt; -&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Analysis of &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;coronary plaque composition and burden.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Fibrotic Plaque&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;- A&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; plaque with density of 131-350 HU.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;High Risk Plaque (HRP)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; - &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;High risk plaque findings include napkin-ring sign, low-attenuation plaque (LAP), positive vessel remodeling, low CT attenuation and spotty calcification.&lt;sup&gt;5,7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;Invasive Coronary Angiography&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 150%;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt; (ICA)&lt;&sol;em&gt; - Invasive procedure done at the time of cardiac catheterization to look at the arteries of the heart and can determine &lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;the presence and extent of CAD.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Low Attenuation Plaque (LAP)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Low density plaque with dark appearance on CCTA and higher lipid content usually defined as&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;attenuation of &amp;lt;30 HU and associated with higher clinical risk. Density is measured in Hounsfield Units (HU) and low attenuation is -50 to 50 HU.&lt;sup&gt;5,8&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;Major Adverse Cardiac Events (MACE)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;Fatal and non-fatal MI. Some studies also include unstable angina requiring hospitalization or revascularization.&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Non-Calcified Plaque (NCP)&lt;&sol;em&gt; - A lower density plaque, often earlier in development and associated with higher clinical risk with density of 50-130 HU.&lt;sup&gt;5,8 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;There is no discernible calcification present.&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Nonobstructive CAD&lt;&sol;em&gt; - CAD with &amp;lt;50% stenosis.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Obstructive CAD&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; - &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;CAD with &amp;gt;50% stenosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Plaque&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;- The presence of tissue structures &amp;ge; 1mm&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt; within or adjacent to the coronary artery lumen, identified and at least 2 independent planes, that can be distinguished from the surrounding tissues (epicardial fat) and the lumen.&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Quantitative Coronary Plaque Analysis (QCPA)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; - &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Imaging&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;technique that provides objective and reproducible measurements of coronary artery dimensions and composition of the atherosclerotic plaques.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Quantitative Coronary Topography (QCT)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; - &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt;CT scan imaging that provides objective and reproducible measurements of the coronary artery dimensions and degree of stenosis per vessel.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%; background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Provider Qualifications&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Medicare Program Integrity Manual states services will be considered medically reasonable and necessary only if performed by appropriately trained providers.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Patient safety and quality of care mandate that healthcare professionals who interpret CCTA and QCPA and AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA are appropriately trained and&sol;or credentialed by a formal residency&sol;fellowship program. Credentialing or privileges are required for procedures performed in inpatient and outpatient settings.&lt;sup&gt;4&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;All aspects of care must be within the provider&amp;rsquo;s medical licensure and scope of practice. Reimbursement for procedures utilizing imaging techniques may be made to providers who meet training requirements for the procedures in this policy only if their respective state allows such in their practice act and formally licenses or certifies the practitioner to use and interpret these imaging modalities. At a minimum, training must cover and develop an understanding of anatomy and drug pharmacodynamics and kinetics as well as proficiency in diagnosis and management of disease, the technical performance of the procedure, and utilization of the required associated imaging modalities. &lt;&sol;span&gt;Supervision, interpretation, and reports shall&sol;must be performed by a physician with the advanced training requirements and or credentialing for CCTA and AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA. &lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The technical and professional portions must meet the criteria for performance for CCTA (L&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;33423&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;). &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Providers must also meet the U.S Food and Drug Administration (FDA) requirements which includes &amp;ldquo;The software is not intended to replace the skill and judgement of a qualified medical practitioner and should only be used by people who have been appropriately trained in the software&amp;rsquo;s functions, capabilities and limitations.&amp;rdquo;&lt;sup&gt;9&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;Radiology technicians must also meet all training requirements for performance of AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Notice: &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Services performed for any given diagnosis must meet all the indications and limitations stated in this LCD, the general requirements for medical necessity as stated in CMS payment policy manuals, all existing CMS national coverage determinations (NCD), and all Medicare payment rules.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;42 CFR &amp;sect;410.32(a) Diagnostic x-ray tests, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;80 Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, &amp;sect;80.1.1 Certification Changes&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p style=&quot;background: white; text-align: justify;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Current molecular diagnostic tests that identify individuals with upper gastrointestinal metaplasia, dysplasia, and neoplasia are non-covered by this contractor. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;background: white; text-align: justify;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;We expect these types of tests to meet the following criteria:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary is being actively managed for chronic gastroesophageal reflex disease (GERD) and&sol;or non-dysplastic Barrett&amp;rsquo;s esophagus (NDBE); and&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;They also have at least three additional risk factors for Barrett&amp;rsquo;s esophagus (BE) as described in nationally recognized guidelines.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary has not been previously diagnosed with dysplasia or esophageal carcinoma; and &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary is tested no more than recommended by established national or society guidelines; and&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The test identifies patients with dysplastic disease that may benefit from endoscopic treatment or surveillance, or patients with non-dysplastic disease who may benefit from surveillance;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The test results will be used in determining treatment or management of the beneficiary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary is within the population for which the test was developed and validated. The laboratory providing the test is responsible for clearly indicating to treating clinicians the population and indication for test use.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The test demonstrates analytical validity (AV) including an analytical and clinical validation for any given measured analytes, and has demonstrated equivalence or superiority for sensitivity or specificity of detecting dysplasia to other already accepted methods for the same intended use measuring the same or comparable analytes.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;Clinical validity (CV) of any analyte measured must be demonstrated in the published peer-reviewed literature, establishing a clear and significant biological&sol;molecular basis for stratifying patients and subsequently selecting (either&lt;&sol;span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;positively or negatively) their clinical management decision within a clearly defined population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;The test successfully completes a Technical Assessment that will ensure that criteria set in this policy are met to establish the test as Reasonable and Necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;42 CFR &amp;sect;410.32(a) Diagnostic x-ray tests, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;80 Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, &amp;sect;80.1.1 Certification Changes&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This contractor will cover molecular diagnostic tests for use in a beneficiary with an indeterminate or suspicious thyroid nodule when all the following criteria are met:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient:&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;circle&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Has an index nodule that has not been tested with the same or similar assay for the same clinical indication AND&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;square&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The nodule is indeterminate as defined by Bethesda categories III-IV OR&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The nodule is Bethesda category V and molecular testing may aid in further stratifying the type of malignancy.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;If the patient has multiple nodules, concurrent or reflex testing may be medically necessary, provided the above criteria are also met.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The results of the test will be used to aid in surgical decision making after a consideration of clinical, radiographic and cytologic features.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary is within the population and has the indication for which the test was developed. The laboratory providing the test is responsible for clearly indicating to treating clinicians the population and indication for test use.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test demonstrates analytical validity, including both analytical and clinical validation, on a cohort of patients appropriate for its intended use. If the test relies on an algorithm, the algorithm must be validated in a cohort that is not a development cohort for the algorithm.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test has demonstrated clinical validity and utility in peer-reviewed, published literature, establishing a clear and significant biological&sol;molecular basis for stratifying patients and subsequently selecting (either positively or negatively) a clinical management decision in a clearly defined population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test successfully completes a technical assessment that ensures the test is reasonable and necessary as described above.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The performance characteristics of the test have been demonstrated to be as good or better than currently covered services.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;NOTE: Next Generation Sequencing (NGS) performed to identify genetic variants in samples classified as malignant is not within the scope of this policy but may fall under other established policies.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;This LCD supplements but does not replace, modify or supersede existing Medicare applicable National Coverage Determinations (NCDs) or payment policy rules and regulations for Erythropoiesis Stimulating Agents. All providers who report services for Medicare payment must fully understand and follow all existing laws, regulations, and rules for Medicare payment for Erythropoiesis Stimulating Agents and must properly submit only valid claims for them.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) excludes expenses incurred for items or services which are not reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1881(b)(1) allows payment for services furnished to individuals who have been determined to have end stage renal disease.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, Chapter 1, &amp;sect;30 Drugs and Biologicals&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, Chapter 13, &amp;sect;120 Services and Supplies Furnished &amp;ldquo;Incident to&amp;rdquo; Physician&amp;rsquo;s Services&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;50 Drugs and Biologicals, &amp;sect;50.1 Definition of Drug or Biological, &amp;sect;50.2 Determining Self-Administration of Drug or Biological, &amp;sect;50.3 Incident-to Requirements, &amp;sect;50.4.1 Approved Use of Drug, &amp;sect;50.4.3 Examples of Not Reasonable and Necessary, &amp;sect;50.5.2 Erythropoietin (EPO), &amp;sect;50.5.2.1 Requirements for Medicare Coverage for EPO, and &amp;sect;50.5.2.2 Medicare Coverage of Epoetin Alfa (Procrit) for Preoperative Use&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-03, Medicare National Coverage Determinations (NCD) Manual, Chapter 1, Part 2, &amp;sect;110.21 Erythropoiesis Stimulating Agents (ESAs) in Cancer and Related Neoplastic Conditions&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https:&sol;&sol;www.cms.gov&sol;medicare-coverage-database&sol;details&sol;nca-details.aspx?NCAId=203&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;NCD for ESAs for Cancer and Related Neoplastic Conditions - CAG-00383N (cms.gov)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;a&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https:&sol;&sol;www.cms.gov&sol;medicare-coverage-database&sol;details&sol;nca-details.aspx?NCAId=245&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;National Coverage Analysis (NCA) for Erythropoiesis Stimulating Agents (ESAs) for Treatment of Anemia in Adults with CKD Including Patients on Dialysis and Patients not on Dialysis (CAG-00413N) (cms.gov)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;a&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CR 11244 Discontinuing the Erythropoietin Stimulating Agent (ESA) Monitoring Policy System Edits under the End Stage Renal Dialysis Prospective Payment System (ESRD PPS) Effective 01&sol;01&sol;2020 &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https:&sol;&sol;www.cms.gov&sol;Regulations-and-Guidance&sol;Guidance&sol;Manuals&sol;Downloads&sol;Pub100_20pdf.pdf&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Pub 100-20 - One-Time Notification (PDF) (cms.gov)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;a&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;Erythropoietin (EPO) is naturally produced by the kidneys and stimulates the proliferation of red blood cells (RBCs) in the bone marrow. An erythropoietin stimulating agent (ESA) is a biologically engineered analog of EPO. &lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;ESAs contain the identical (or very similar) amino acid sequence as naturally occurring EPO and have the same biological effect.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 121%; background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;Several chronic conditions, especially chronic renal failure, result in decreased production of or relative resistance to EPO, often causing anemia. Supplementation by synthetic drugs with structures identical or similar to naturally occurring EPO has been proven safe and effective in correcting anemia in certain groups of patients. By elevating or maintaining the RBC level (as demonstrated by the hematocrit (HCT) and&sol;or hemoglobin (Hb) levels), these synthetic analogs can decrease anemia and the need for transfusions.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;Since 2007, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has issued new warnings against target Hb levels above 12 g&sol;dL (HCT of 36%) for &amp;ldquo;all patients.&amp;rdquo; The FDA has also issued specific warnings against off-label use in cancer patients whose anemia is not directly linked to chemotherapy. The FDA has consistently reminded physicians that the &lt;u&gt;main&lt;&sol;u&gt; endpoint in studies for on-label indications has been avoidance of or reduction in transfusions.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;All ESAs covered in this LCD per FDA indications must be administered per &lt;&sol;span&gt;FDA-approved label guidance. Please see the FDA drug label for the FDA-approved indications and dosages. This can be accessed at: &lt;a href=&quot;https:&sol;&sol;labels.fda.gov&sol;&quot;&gt;https:&sol;&sol;labels.fda.gov&sol;&lt;&sol;a&gt;&lt;u&gt;.&lt;&sol;u&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) has issued a National Coverage Determination (NCD) 110.21 for the use of ESAs&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; in cancer and related neoplastic conditions.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%; background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;u&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;Please refer to CMS Internet-Only Manual Publication 100-03, Medicare National Coverage Determinations (NCD) Manual, Chapter 1, Part 2, &amp;sect;110.21 for nationally covered indications related to ESA treatment for anemia secondary to myelosuppressive anticancer chemotherapy in solid tumors, multiple myeloma, lymphoma, and lymphocytic leukemia. This national determination includes dosing information which is not superseded by this LCD. Nationally non-covered indications for which ESA treatment is not reasonable and necessary for beneficiaries with certain clinical conditions is also detailed. The Decision Memo for ESAs for non-renal disease indications (CAG-00383N) provides insight related to the evidence analysis use in promulgating the NCD.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;This LCD does not supersede but does incorporate information from the NCD and covers some additional limited non-cancer related indications per the discretion afforded to Medicare Administrative Contractors (MACs). For specificity as to billing and coding advice that will support the reasonable and necessary nature of ESA administration for various conditions, it is critical that the related billing and coding article to this LCD be reviewed.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;This A&sol;B MAC does recognize the widely variable use of ESAs for a broad spectrum of conditions. The strength and quantity of evidence supporting such uses is also widely variable in terms of quality and volume. As such, this LCD has specified only limited coverage outside of FDA-approved indications. Denials of claims related to this limited coverage may be appealed on a case-by-case basis. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;h3 style=&quot;margin: 7.4pt 0in .0001pt 0in;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11.0pt;&quot;&gt;Covered Indications for ESAs:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;u&gt;&lt;&sol;h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Treatment of significant anemia in patients with non-myeloid malignancies where anemia is specifically due to concomitantly administered chemotherapy;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Treatment of symptomatic anemia related to end-stage renal disease (ESRD) and stages IIIb, IV and V chronic kidney disease (CKD);&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Treatment of anemia induced by AZT (Zidovudine) used in HIV&sol;AIDS therapy;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Treatment of selected patients with anemia related to low prognostic risk myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) and some myeloproliferative neoplasms;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Peri-surgical adjuvant therapy for purposes of allogenic RBC transfusion reduction&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The following causes of anemia should be considered, documented, and corrected before starting or continuing ESA therapy for any of the above covered indications:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Iron deficiency;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Underlying infection, inflammatory or malignant processes;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Underlying hematological disease;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Hemolysis;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Vitamin deficiencies (e.g., folic acid or B12);&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Blood loss-overt or occult;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Aluminum intoxication;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Osteitis fibrosis cystica; or&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Pure RBC aplasia&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;ESA treatment is not reasonable and necessary for beneficiaries with certain clinical conditions, either because of a deleterious effect of the ESA on their underlying disease or because the underlying disease increases their risk of adverse effects related to ESA use. These conditions include:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Any anemia in cancer or cancer treatment patients due to folate deficiency, B-12 deficiency, iron deficiency, hemolysis, bleeding, or bone marrow fibrosis;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The anemia associated with the treatment of acute and chronic myelogenous leukemias (chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), acute myeloid leukemia (AML)), or erythroid cancers;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The anemia of cancer not related to cancer treatment;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Any anemia associated only with radiotherapy;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Prophylactic use to prevent chemotherapy-induced anemia;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Prophylactic use to reduce tumor hypoxia;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Patients with EPO-type resistance due to neutralizing antibodies; and&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Anemia due to cancer treatment if patients have uncontrolled hypertension.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Non-ESRD ESA services are not considered reasonable and necessary within the context of other medical conditions for which resolution would be&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; reasonably expected prior to starting or continuing ESA administration. Such conditions would include, but not be limited to iron&sol;vitamin B12&sol;folate deficiencies, G6PD deficiency, pyridoxine deficiency, various forms of hemolysis, hereditary spherocytosis, and pure red cell aplasias. The presence of any of these conditions would reduce the therapeutic impact and effectiveness of the ESA. Additionally, the presence of unspecified anemia suggests appropriate evaluation, to determine the nature of the treated anemia, has not been completed.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;There are very rare patients whose cardiac, pulmonary or other medical conditions warrant the use of ESAs to maintain a Hb&sol;HCT higher than the FDA target levels discussed in this LCD. Documentation to support this practice must be available upon request. (This instruction does not apply to ESA therapy for anemia related to cancer chemotherapy, which follows the rules mandated by the NCD 110.21.)&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;During therapy with an ESA, many patients will require supplemental iron. For these patients, stores of iron should be regularly monitored. Reference the related billing and coding article for further detail.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;For patients receiving chemotherapy for non-myeloid malignancies, the goal of therapy is to avoid transfusions. ESA therapy will be reimbursed only when the Hb is less than 10 g&sol;dL or the HCT is less than 30%.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;For all other indications, the goal of therapy is to maintain a stable Hb and HCT, with target ranges of 10-12 g&sol;dL and 30-36% respectively. Doses must be titrated according to the patient&amp;rsquo;s response. ESA therapy need not be stopped completely simply due to the achievement of the target Hb and&sol;or HCT. However, judicious, appropriately timed dose adjustments are expected to prevent inappropriate increases in Hb and HCT levels.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The likelihood of anemia associated with EPO deficiency increases as renal failure progresses and the diseased kidneys are unable to produce sufficient amounts of EPO. The anemia of CKD should not be confused with the anemia of chronic disease. In the latter, inflammatory cytokines suppress the endogenous production of EPO&amp;nbsp;and erythropoiesis directly. Measurable levels of circulating cytokines may be found in stable dialysis patients, but, in the absence of inflammation, do not adversely affect the action of ESAs. In patients with impaired renal function and a normochromic, normocytic anemia, it is rare for the serum EPO level to be elevated. Therefore, measurement of EPO levels in such patients is not likely to guide clinical decision making or ESA therapy. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;ESAs may be administered by intravenous or subcutaneous routes. An intravenous route is generally recommended for an ESRD indication. Please see the related billing and coding article for details related to required modifier use on claims for ESA administration reporting.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Coverage Criteria: &lt;&sol;u&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;(Review the related billing and coding article for further detail regarding documentation)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: upper-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For ESRD patients on dialysis&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Diagnosis of ESRD&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Anemia of ESRD with a Hb &amp;lt; 10 gm&sol;dL or a HCT &amp;lt; 30% at initiation of therapy&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Most recent creatinine within the past month prior to initiation or next dosing of ESA&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Use of an ESA that is FDA-approved for this indication&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For CKD patients NOT on dialysis&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Anemia of CKD with a Hb &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL or a HCT &amp;lt; 30% at initiation of therapy&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Most recent creatinine within the past month prior to initiation or next dosing of ESA&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) less than 45 mL&sol;min&sol;1.73 m&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Use of an ESA that is FDA-approved for this indication&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For patients with non-myeloid malignancies with anemia due to chemotherapy&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This LCD does not replace, modify or supersede existing Medicare applicable NCDs. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Hb level immediately prior to initiation or maintenance of ESA treatment is &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL (or HCT is &amp;lt; 30%)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Use of an ESA that is FDA-approved for this indication&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The starting dose for ESA treatment is the recommended FDA label starting dose.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Maintenance of ESA therapy is the starting dose if the Hb level remains below 10 g&sol;dL (or HCT is &amp;lt; 30%) 4 weeks after initiation of therapy and the rise in Hb is &amp;gt; 1g&sol;dL (HCT &amp;gt; 3%).&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For patients whose Hb rises &amp;lt; 1g&sol;dL (HCT rise &amp;lt; 3%) compared to pretreatment baseline over 4 weeks of treatment and whose Hb remains &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL afterwards (or HCT &amp;lt; 30%), the recommended FDA label starting dose may be increased once by 25%. Continued use of the drug is not reasonable and necessary if the Hb rises &amp;lt; 1g&sol;dL (HCT rise &amp;lt; 3%) compared to pretreatment baseline by 8 weeks of treatment.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Continued administration of the drug is not reasonable and necessary if there is a rapid rise in Hb &amp;gt; 1 g&sol;dl (HCT &amp;gt;3%) over 2 weeks of treatment unless the Hb or HCT remains below or subsequently falls to &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL or 30% respectively. Continuation and reinstitution of ESA therapy must include a dose reduction of 25% from the previously administered dose.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;ESA treatment duration for each course of chemotherapy includes the 8 weeks following the final dose of myelosuppressive chemotherapy in a chemotherapy regimen.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 121%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For patients with anemia related to AZT treatment for HIV&sol;AIDS&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Anemia with Hb &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL or a HCT &amp;lt; 30% at initiation of therapy&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Use of an ESA that is FDA-approved for this indication&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;An AZT dose &lt;u&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&sol;u&gt; 4200 mg&sol;week &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;An endogenous baseline pre-transfusion serum EPO (sEPO) level &lt;u&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&sol;u&gt; 500 mU&sol;mL&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For Peri-surgical adjuvant therapy to reduce allogenic transfusion: &lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Undergoing planned elective major hip or knee surgery&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Pre-surgical anemia with Hb between 10 and 13 g&sol;dL at least 3 weeks prior to surgery&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Use of an ESA that is FDA-approved for this indication&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Not a candidate for autologous blood transfusion&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Expectation for peri-operative blood loss of 2 units or more&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Previous evaluation to ensure that the existing anemia is likely due to chronic disease rather than another reversible condition&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;In addition to the FDA-labeled indications above, ESAs are covered for the following off-label indications. At this time, there is no clearly established dosing regimen for off-label indications.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For patients with symptomatic anemia related to very low, low or low score intermediate risk MDS&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Revised International Prognostic Scoring System (IPSS-R)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; score correlating to very low, low or a low score intermediate risk or IPSS score of low or intermediate-1 risk or WPSS of very low, low or intermediate risk*&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Pretreatment EPO levels of 500 or less&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Documented anemia-related symptoms&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; such as fatigue, pallor, infection, bleeding or bruising or transfusion dependence &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Documentation of a reasonable expectancy of longer survival with a reduced need for transfusion support&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Diagnosis of MDS confirmed by bone marrow aspiration and&sol;or biopsy report &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Anemia with Hb &amp;lt; 10 g&sol;dL or a HCT of &amp;lt; 30% at initiation of therapy. If after 2 months of treatment, there is no significant increase in Hb&sol;HCT and&sol;or a significant decrease in transfusion requirements, EPO analog therapy should be stopped.&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations Specified by CMS and&sol;or This A&sol;B MAC&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;See the related billing and coding article for further detail regarding necessary coding information.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;* This A&sol;B MAC will monitor new developing prognostic stratification systems, especially those anticipated to be based on mutational analysis and will adjust the billing and coding article as needed in this regard.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(7) excludes routine physical examinations.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(13)(C) addresses routine foot care.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;290 Foot Care&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-03, Medicare National Coverage Determinations Manual, Chapter 1, Part 1, &amp;sect;70.2.1 Services Provided for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Diabetic Sensory Neuropathy with Loss of Protective Sensation (aka Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-08, Medicare Program Integrity Manual, Chapter 13, &amp;sect;13.5.4 Reasonable and Necessary Provisions in LCDs&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Nail surgery is often performed to remove benign and malignant nail tumors, relieve pain caused by ingrown and traumatized nails, manage disease processes, and diagnose challenging lesions and dystrophies.&lt;sup&gt;8&lt;&sol;sup&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;An ingrown nail (onychocryptosis) is a condition which results in the growth of the nail edge into the surrounding soft tissue. Ingrown toenails present with a varying degree of inflammation and edema of the nail folds. This often results in a draining, foul-smelling lesion with hypertrophy of the involved nail fold; however, the most common accompanying symptom is pain. This condition most commonly occurs in the great toes and may require surgical treatment in moderate to severe cases.&lt;sup&gt;7,10,13&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Ingrown toenails account for approximately 20% of foot problems presenting to primary care.&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;&sol;sup&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Other conditions may also require avulsion of part or all the nail. These conditions may include but are not limited to onychomycosis, subungual hematoma, trauma, onychogryphosis, psoriasis, lichen planus, congenital nail dystrophies, and tumors.&lt;sup&gt;1,2,5,12,14&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Onychauxis, which can result from nail fungus, psoriasis, or other conditions, may cause onycholysis (separation of the nail plate from the nail bed). In the case of a moderate to severe symptomatic dystrophic nail plate, surgical intervention may be needed.&lt;sup&gt;5,7,12&lt;&sol;sup&gt; This LCD addresses the conditions for which nail avulsion may be considered reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Surgical techniques for the treatment of nail pathology include complete or partial nail avulsion. Nail avulsion requires adequate anesthesia for optimal results.&lt;sup&gt;11 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;Generally, this procedure is performed under local anesthesia and involves the separation and removal of a border of the nail or removal of the entire nail from the nail bed to the eponychium.&lt;sup&gt;8&lt;&sol;sup&gt; When possible, partial nail plate avulsion is preferred to complete avulsion, because it minimizes trauma to the adjacent tissues.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Nail avulsions usually offer only temporary relief for ingrown toenails. The nail often grows back to its original thickness, and the offending margin again may become problematic, resulting in a need for another nail avulsion. Often, the preferred course of treatment may be a partial or complete nail avulsion performed with a matrixectomy, which is destruction or permanent removal of the matrix to prevent the nail from regrowing.&lt;sup&gt;6,9,13,17&lt;&sol;sup&gt; The matrixectomy can be performed either chemically or surgically. When nail avulsion is performed without matrixectomy, the nail will regrow from the matrix.&lt;sup&gt;4&lt;&sol;sup&gt; A fingernail takes approximately 4&amp;ndash;6 months to regrow, and a toenail takes approximately 8&amp;ndash;12 months to regrow.&lt;sup&gt;1,13,14,16&lt;&sol;sup&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Wedge excision of skin of the nail fold is a procedure designed to relieve pressure on the nail&sol;soft tissue and requires an excision of a wedge of the soft granulation tissue and ingrown nail from the affected side (medial and&sol;or lateral) of the toe or finger.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Services will be considered medically reasonable and necessary when all aspects of care are within the scope of practice of the provider&amp;rsquo;s professional licensure, when performed according to the supervision requirements per state scope of practice laws, and when all procedures are performed by appropriately trained providers in the appropriate setting.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Covered Indications&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Surgical treatment of nails is covered for the following indications:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Symptomatic onychocryptosis (ingrown fingernails or toenails)&lt;sup&gt;6,7,10,13,15,17&lt;&sol;sup&gt;;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Subungual abscess and&sol;or hematoma after a failed puncture aspiration;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Complicated injury of the toes or fingers involving the nail component that is severe enough to require removal of the nail to evaluate the stability of the nail bed or to release a subungual hematoma&lt;sup&gt;14&lt;&sol;sup&gt;;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Severe or recurrent fungal nail infection that has failed to respond to usual, less invasive treatment (e.g., pharmacological treatment, debridement);&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Onychogryphosis or onychauxis&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Congenital or acquired nail dystrophies that jeopardize the integrity of the finger or toe&lt;sup&gt;1,8&lt;&sol;sup&gt;;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Diagnosis of suspected lichen planus or psoriasis of the fingernail or toenail&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Subungual and periungual tumors&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For a medically reasonable and necessary repeat nail excision on the same finger or toe. The medical record documentation must be specific as to the indication, such as ingrown nail of the opposite border or new significant pathology on the same border recently treated;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;For a medically reasonable and necessary repeat nail avulsion of the same toenail or fingernail performed more often than every 32 weeks (8 months) for toenails or every 16 weeks (4 months) for fingernails. The medical record documentation must be specific as to the indication, such as ingrown nail of the opposite border or new significant pathology on the same border recently treated.&lt;sup&gt;1,13,14,16&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Limitations&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The following indications are non-covered and are considered not medically reasonable and necessary:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Trimming, cutting, clipping or debridement of nails;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Removing small chips or wedges of the nail or skin that does not require local anesthesia does not constitute surgical treatment of a nail&lt;sup&gt;1,13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Simple treatment of ingrown toenails (e.g., trimming, cutting, clipping of the distal unattached nail margins) does not constitute surgical treatment of nails;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul type=&quot;disc&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Surgical treatment of asymptomatic conditions&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(D) addresses services that are determined to be investigational or experimental.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
