indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This is a limited coverage policy for next-generation sequencing (NGS) assays performed on solid tumor cell-free deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) in plasma, from here on called &amp;ldquo;liquid biopsies.&amp;rdquo;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Criteria for Coverage &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; is covered only when &lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;all&lt;&sol;u&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt; of the following conditions are met:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Patient has been diagnosed with a recurrent, relapsed, refractory, metastatic, or advanced solid tumor that did not originate from the central nervous system. Patients who would meet all of the indications on the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) label for &lt;a href=&quot;https:&sol;&sol;www.accessdata.fda.gov&sol;drugsatfda_docs&sol;label&sol;2018&sol;211710s000lbl.pdf&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer&quot;&gt;larotrectinib&lt;&sol;a&gt; if they are found to have a neurotrophic receptor tyrosine kinase&amp;nbsp;(NTRK) mutation may be considered to have advanced cancer, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Patient has not previously been tested with the Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; test for the same genetic content. For a patient who has been tested previously using Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; for cancer, that patient may not be tested again unless there is clinical evidence that the cancer has evolved wherein testing would be performed for different genetic content. Specifically, in patients with previously tested cancer, who have evidence of new malignant growth despite response to a prior targeted therapy, that growth may be considered to be sufficiently genetically different to require additional genetic testing, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Patient is untreated for the cancer being tested, or the patient is not responding to treatment (e.g., progression or new lesions on treatment), &lt;strong&gt;and &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient has decided to seek further cancer treatment with the following conditions:&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient is a candidate for further treatment with a drug that is either FDA-approved for that patient&amp;rsquo;s cancer, or has a National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) 1 or NCCN 2A recommendation for that patient&amp;rsquo;s cancer, &lt;strong&gt;and &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The FDA-approved indication or NCCN recommendation is based upon information about the presence or absence of a genetic biomarker tested for in the Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; assay, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Tissue-based, comprehensive genomic profiling (CGP) is infeasible (e.g., quantity not sufficient for tissue-based CGP or invasive biopsy is medically contraindicated) &lt;strong&gt;or&lt;&sol;strong&gt; specifically in NSCLC Tissue-based CGP has shown no actionable mutations. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;If no alteration is detected by Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; or if circulating tumor deoxyribonucleic acid (ctDNA) is insufficient&sol;not detected, tissue-based genotyping should be considered.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Other liquid biopsies will be covered for the same indications if they display similar performance in their intended used applications to Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A wide array of cancer treatments have developed ranging from surgery to medications. One of the newer approaches to the medical treatment of cancer has been to use drugs based on genetic features of a malignancy. While many patients will not benefit from genetic testing to select treatment, for those whose cancers have select biomarkers, the treatment of choice often includes therapy targeting that specific biomarker or therapy being avoided because of a biomarker.&lt;sup&gt;1-13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In spite of the importance of actionable biomarker identification in cancer, research has shown that many patients do not receive genetic testing for the presence of actionable mutations in their cancers, and there are geographic disparities in testing with patients in rural areas and those receiving care at community treatment centers being less likely to receive testing.&lt;sup&gt;14-16&amp;nbsp; &lt;&sol;sup&gt;In addition, logistical challenges to testing such as adequate tissue and the availability of any tissue have been identified as barriers to tissue-based genomic testing.&lt;sup&gt;15&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Additionally, even among patients whose cancers were genomically profiled at diagnosis and found to have a mutation for which they are receiving targeted treatment, resistance to the initial targeted treatment may emerge. For some patients, the identification of a new mutation, not present in the original tissue sample and found in the blood, may allow the selection of a new targeted life-prolonging therapy.&lt;sup&gt;17&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CMS National Coverage Policy&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(I)(5)(D) The 21st Century Cures Act of 2016 (Public Law 114-255) added language directing the Secretary of the Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) to improve the transparency of the LCD process.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(D) Items and services related to research and experimentation.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(7) states Medicare will not cover any services or procedures associated with routine physical checkups.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1833 (e) prohibits Medicare payment for any claim which lacks the necessary information to process the claim.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;42 CFR &amp;sect;410.32 indicates that diagnostic tests may only be ordered by the treating physician (or other treating practitioner acting within the scope of his or her license and Medicare requirements).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Protecting Access to Medicare Act (PAMA) of 2014, Section 218(b), established a new program to increase the rate of appropriate advanced diagnostic imaging services provided to Medicare beneficiaries&lt;&sol;p&gt;
