indication:
&lt;p&gt;This is a limited coverage policy for next-generation sequencing (NGS) assays performed on solid tumor cell-free DNA in plasma, from here on called &amp;ldquo;liquid biopsies.&amp;rdquo;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Criteria for Coverage&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; is covered only when &lt;strong&gt;all&lt;&sol;strong&gt; of the following conditions are met:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient has been diagnosed with a recurrent, relapsed, refractory, metastatic, or advanced solid tumor that did not originate from the central nervous system. Patients who would meet all of the indications on the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) label for larotrectinib if they are found to have a neurotrophic receptor tyrosine kinase (NTRK) mutation may be considered to have advanced cancer, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient has not previously been tested with the Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; test for the same genetic content. For a patient who has been tested previously using Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; for cancer, that patient may not be tested again unless there is clinical evidence that the cancer has evolved wherein testing would be performed for a different genetic content.&amp;nbsp;Specifically, in patients with previously tested cancer, who have evidence of new malignant growth, despite response to a prior targeted therapy, that growth may be considered to be sufficiently genetically different to require additional genetic testing, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient is untreated for the cancer being tested or the patient is not responding to treatment (e.g. progression or new lesions on treatment), &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The patient has decided to seek further cancer treatment with the following conditions:
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The patient is a candidate for further treatment with a drug that is either FDA-approved for that patient&amp;rsquo;s cancer, or has a National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) or NCCN 2A recommendation for that patient&amp;rsquo;s cancer, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The FDA-approved indication or NCCN recommendation is based upon information about the presence or absence of a genetic biomarker tested for in the Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; assay &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tissue-based, comprehensive genomic profiling (CGP) is infeasible (e.g., quantity not sufficient for tissue-based CGP or invasive biopsy is medically contraindicated) or specifically in NSCLC Tissue-based CGP has shown no actionable mutations.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If no alteration is detected by Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt; or if circulating tumor deoxyribonucleic acid (ctDNA) is insufficient&sol;not detected, tissue-based genotyping should be considered.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Other liquid biopsies will be covered for the same indications if they display similar performance in their intended use applications to Guardant360&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

