&lt;&sol;tbody&gt;
&lt;&sol;table&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Coronary Computed Tomography Angiography (CCTA)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;- a non-invasive test using advanced computed tomography angiography imaging to view the tissues and blood vessels of the heart. This can be used to determine the presence and extent of CAD. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Coronary Plaque Analysis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;strong&gt;(CPA)- Analysis of &lt;&sol;strong&gt;coronary plaque composition and burden.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Fibrotic Plaque- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;a plaque with density of 131-350 HU.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;High Risk Plaque (HRP)- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;High risk plaque findings include napkin-ring sign, low-attenuation plaque, positive vessel remodeling, low CT attenuation and spotty calcification.&lt;sup&gt;5,7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Invasive Coronary Angiography (ICA)- &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Invasive procedure done at the time of cardiac catheterization to look at the arteries of the heart and can determine &lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;the presence and extent of CAD.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Low Attenuation Plaque (LAP)-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Low density plaque with dark appearance on CCTA and higher lipid content usually defined as&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;attenuation of &amp;lt;30 Hounsfield unit and associated with higher clinical risk. Density is measured in Hounsfield Units (HU) and low attenuation is -50 to 50HU.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;5,8&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Major Adverse Cardiac Events (MACE)- &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Fatal and non-fatal myocardial infarction. Some studies also include unstable angina requiring hospitalization or revascularization.&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Non-Calcified Plaque (NCP) &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&amp;ndash; A lower density plaque, often earlier in development and associated with higher clinical risk with density of 50-130 HU.&lt;sup&gt;5,8&lt;&sol;sup&gt; There is no discernible calcification present.&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nonobstructive CAD&lt;&sol;strong&gt;- CAD with &amp;lt;50% stenosis.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Obstructive CAD- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;CAD with &amp;gt;50% stenosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Plaque-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; The presence of tissue structures &amp;ge; 1mm&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt; within or adjacent to the coronary artery lumen, identified in at least two independent planes, that can be distinguished from the surrounding tissues (epicardial fat) and the lumen.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Quantitative Coronary Plaque Analysis (QCPA)-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; Imaging technique that provides objective and reproducible measurements of coronary artery dimensions and composition of the atherosclerotic plaques.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Quantitative Coronary Topography (QCT)- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;CT scan imaging that provides objective and reproducible measurements of the coronary artery dimensions and degree of stenosis per vessel.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 11.4pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Provider Qualifications&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;The Medicare Program Integrity Manual states services will be considered medically reasonable and necessary only if performed by appropriately trained providers.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Patient safety and quality of care mandate that healthcare professionals who interpret CCTA and QCPA and AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA are appropriately trained and&sol;or credentialed by a formal residency&sol;fellowship program. Credentialing or privileges are required for procedures performed in inpatient and outpatient settings.&lt;sup&gt;4&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.25pt;&quot;&gt;All aspects of care must be within the provider&amp;rsquo;s medical licensure and scope of practice. Reimbursement for procedures utilizing imaging techniques may be made to providers who meet training requirements for the procedures in this policy only if their respective state allows such in their practice act and formally licenses or certifies the practitioner to use and interpret these imaging modalities. At a minimum, training must cover and develop an understanding of anatomy and drug pharmacodynamics and kinetics as well as proficiency in diagnosis and management of disease, the technical performance of the procedure, and utilization of the required associated imaging modalities. Supervision, interpretation, and reports shall&sol;must be performed by a physician with the advanced training requirements and or credentialing for CCTA and AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA. The technical and professional portions must meet the criteria for performance for CCTA (&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;L33559&lt;&sol;span&gt;).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.25pt;&quot;&gt;Providers must also meet the FDA requirements which includes &amp;ldquo;The software is not intended to replace the skill and judgment of a qualified medical practitioner and should only be used by people who have been appropriately trained in the software&amp;rsquo;s functions, capabilities and limitations.&amp;rdquo;&lt;sup&gt;9&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Radiology technicians must also meet all training requirements for performance of AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.25pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Notice: &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Services performed for any given diagnosis must meet all the indications and limitations stated in this LCD, the general requirements for medical necessity as stated in CMS payment policy manuals, all existing CMS national coverage determinations, and all Medicare payment rules.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury or to improve the functioning of a malformed body member.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(D) addresses services that are determined to be investigational or experimental.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, Chapter 16, &amp;sect;10 General Exclusions from Coverage&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-03, Medicare National Coverage Determinations (NCD) Manual, Chapter 1, Part 4, &amp;sect;270.3 Blood-Derived Products for Chronic Non-healing Wounds.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Effective August 2, 2012, updated 4&sol;13&sol;21, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) has determined that platelet-rich plasma (PRP) &amp;ndash; an autologous blood-derived product, will be covered for the treatment of chronic non-healing diabetic wounds under section 1862(a)(1)(A) for a duration of 20 weeks, when prepared by devices who FDA cleared. Indications include the management of exuding cutaneous wounds, such as diabetic ulcers.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-04, Medicare Claims Processing Manual, Chapter 23, &amp;sect;30A Physician&amp;rsquo;s Services&lt;&sol;p&gt;
