&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Coronary Computed Tomography Angiography (CCTA)&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;- a non-invasive test using advanced computed tomography angiography imaging to view the tissues and blood vessels of the heart. This can be used to determine the presence and extent of CAD. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Coronary Plaque Analysis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;strong&gt;(CPA)- Analysis of &lt;&sol;strong&gt;coronary plaque composition and burden.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Fibrotic Plaque- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;a plaque with density of 131-350 HU.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;High Risk Plaque (HRP)- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;High risk plaque findings include napkin-ring sign, low-attenuation plaque, positive vessel remodeling, low CT attenuation and spotty calcification.&lt;sup&gt;5,7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Invasive Coronary Angiography (ICA)- &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Invasive procedure done at the time of cardiac catheterization to look at the arteries of the heart and can determine &lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;the presence and extent of CAD.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Low Attenuation Plaque (LAP)-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Low density plaque with dark appearance on CCTA and higher lipid content usually defined as&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;attenuation of &amp;lt;30 Hounsfield unit and associated with higher clinical risk. Density is measured in Hounsfield Units (HU) and low attenuation is -50 to 50HU.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;5,8&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Major Adverse Cardiac Events (MACE)- &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Fatal and non-fatal myocardial infarction. Some studies also include unstable angina requiring hospitalization or revascularization.&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Non-Calcified Plaque (NCP) &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&amp;ndash; A lower density plaque, often earlier in development and associated with higher clinical risk with density of 50-130 HU.&lt;sup&gt;5,8&lt;&sol;sup&gt; There is no discernible calcification present.&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nonobstructive CAD&lt;&sol;strong&gt;- CAD with &amp;lt;50% stenosis.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Obstructive CAD- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;CAD with &amp;gt;50% stenosis&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Plaque-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; The presence of tissue structures &amp;ge; 1mm&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt; within or adjacent to the coronary artery lumen, identified and at least two independent planes, that can be distinguished from the surrounding tissues (epicardial fat) and the lumen.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Quantitative Coronary Plaque Analysis (QCPA)-&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; Imaging technique that provides objective and reproducible measurements of coronary artery dimensions and composition of the atherosclerotic plaques.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Quantitative Coronary Topography (QCT)- &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;CT scan imaging that provides objective and reproducible measurements of the coronary artery dimensions and degree of stenosis per vessel.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 11.4pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Provider Qualifications&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;The Medicare Program Integrity Manual states services will be considered medically reasonable and necessary only if performed by appropriately trained providers.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Patient safety and quality of care mandate that healthcare professionals who interpret CCTA and QCPA and AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA are appropriately trained and&sol;or credentialed by a formal residency&sol;fellowship program. Credentialing or privileges are required for procedures performed in inpatient and outpatient settings.&lt;sup&gt;4&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.25pt;&quot;&gt;All aspects of care must be within the provider&amp;rsquo;s medical licensure and scope of practice. Reimbursement for procedures utilizing imaging techniques may be made to providers who meet training requirements for the procedures in this policy only if their respective state allows such in their practice act and formally licenses or certifies the practitioner to use and interpret these imaging modalities. At a minimum, training must cover and develop an understanding of anatomy and drug pharmacodynamics and kinetics as well as proficiency in diagnosis and management of disease, the technical performance of the procedure, and utilization of the required associated imaging modalities. Supervision, interpretation, and reports shall&sol;must be performed by a physician with the advanced training requirements and or credentialing for CCTA and AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA. The technical and professional portions must meet the criteria for performance for CCTA (&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 107%;&quot;&gt;L33947&lt;&sol;span&gt;).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.25pt;&quot;&gt;Providers must also meet the FDA requirements which includes &amp;ldquo;The software is not intended to replace the skill and judgment of a qualified medical practitioner and should only be used by people who have been appropriately trained in the software&amp;rsquo;s functions, capabilities and limitations.&amp;rdquo;&lt;sup&gt;9&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Radiology technicians must also meet all training requirements for performance of AI-QCT&sol;AI-CPA.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.25pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Notice: &lt;&sol;strong&gt;Services performed for any given diagnosis must meet all the indications and limitations stated in this LCD, the general requirements for medical necessity as stated in CMS payment policy manuals, all existing CMS national coverage determinations, and all Medicare payment rules.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal; background: white;&quot;&gt;Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a radiation-free, noninvasive technique used to produce high-quality sectional images of the inside of the body in multiple planes. MRI uses natural magnetic properties of the hydrogen atoms in the body that emit radiofrequency signals when exposed to radio waves within a strong magnetic field. These signals are processed and converted by a computer into high-resolution, three-dimensional, tomographic images. Images and resolution produced by MRI is quite detailed. For some MRI, contrast materials (e.g., gadolinium, gadoteridol, non-ionic and low-osmolar contrast media, ionic and high-osmolar contrast media) are used to enable visualization of a body system or body structure.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;MRI provides superior tissue contrast when compared to a computerized tomography (CT) scan, can image in multiple planes, is not affected by bone artifact, provides vascular imaging capability, and makes use of safer contrast media (gadolinium chelate agents).&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Its major disadvantage over a CT scan is the longer scanning time required for study, making it less useful for emergency evaluations. Contraindications include patients with implanted neurostimulators or cochlear implants. Potential contraindications may include patients with cardiac pacemakers (refer to the CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-03, Medicare National Coverage Determinations (NCD) Manual, Chapter 1, Part 4, &amp;sect;220.2), metal fragments in the eye, magnetic ocular implants or patients with older ferromagnetic intracranial aneurysm clips. All these objects may be potentially displaced when exposed to the powerful magnetic fields used in MRI.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;MRI of the orbit, face and&sol;or neck may be considered medically reasonable and necessary when used to diagnose and characterize pathology of the eye, nasopharynx, oropharynx, and neck including tumors, infection, soft tissue pathologies, and congenital abnormalities. In cases involving trauma to the orbit, face and&sol;or neck, a CT scan is frequently superior to MRI for assessing injury.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In many instances, ordering an MRI of the brain, in addition to an MRI of the orbit, face and&sol;or neck, may be medically necessary on the same day. The medical record should document the medical necessity for these 2 procedures being performed on the same day.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Purpose&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Urine drug testing (UDT) provides timely, objective, and actionable information to clinicians by identifying the presence or absence of drugs of potential abuse in the body to assist the clinician in making treatment decisions.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This policy details:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;The appropriate indications and allowed number of UDTs billed over time for safe medication management of prescribed substances in risk stratified pain management patients and&sol;or in identifying and treating substance use disorders (SUDs);&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Designates documentation, by the clinician caring for the beneficiary in the beneficiary&amp;rsquo;s medical record, of medical necessity for, and testing ordered on an individual patient basis;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Provides an overview of presumptive UDT and definitive UDT testing by various methodologies.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This policy addresses UDT for Medicare patients only.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Definitions&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As used in this document, the following terminology relates to the basic forms of UDT:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Presumptive&sol;Qualitative Drug Testing &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;(hereafter called &quot;presumptive&quot; UDT) &amp;ndash; Covered when medically necessary to immediately determine the presence or absence of drugs or drug classes in a urine sample. Presumptive UDT results are expressed as negative or positive or as a numerical result; includes competitive immunoassays (IA) and thin layer chromatography.&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Definitive&sol;Quantitative&sol;Confirmation Testing &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;(hereafter called &amp;ldquo;definitive&amp;rdquo; UDT) &amp;ndash; Covered when clinically indicated and medically reasonable and necessary based on this Local Coverage Determination (LCD) to identify specific medications, illicit substances and metabolites. Definitive UDT reports the results of analytes absent or present typically in concentrations such as ng&sol;mL; definitive methods include but are not limited to &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Gas Chromatography coupled with Mass Spectrometry&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;GC-MS) and &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Liquid Chromatography coupled with Mass Spectrometry&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; (LC-MS&sol;MS) testing methods.&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Specimen Validity Testing &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;- Urine specimen testing to ensure that it is consistent with normal human urine and has not been adulterated or substituted, may include, but is not limited to pH, specific gravity, oxidants and creatinine. This however is quality assurance, not a Medicare benefit, and thus not separately payable by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Immunoassay (IA) &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;- Ordered by clinicians primarily to identify the presence or absence of drug classes and some specific drugs; biochemical tests that measure the presence above a cutoff level of a substance (drug) with the use of an antibody; read by photometric technology.&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Point of Care Testing (POCT) &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&amp;ndash; Covered when medically necessary by clinicians caring for the beneficiary for immediate test results for the immediate management of the beneficiary; available when the beneficiary and physician are in the same location; IA test method that primarily identifies drug classes and a few specific drugs; platform consists of cups, dipsticks, cassettes, or strips; read by the human eye, or read by instrument assisted direct optical observation.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Standing Orders &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;- Test request for a specific patient representing repetitive testing to monitor a condition or disease for a limited number of sequential visits; individualized orders for certain patients for pre-determined tests based on historical use, risk, and community trend patient profiles; clinician can alter the standing order. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;*Note: A profile is developed based on specific characteristics of a specific patient, while a panel is a general non-specific group of tests that may have unnecessary tests for the specific patient being treated.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Blanket Orders &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;- Test request that is not for a specific patient; rather, it is an identical order for all patients in a clinician&amp;rsquo;s practice without individualized decision making at every visit.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reflex Testing &lt;&sol;strong&gt;- Laboratory testing that is performed &quot;reflexively&quot; after initial test results to identify further diagnostic information essential to patient care. This testing is not necessarily based on a specific physician&#39;s order.&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Drug Testing Methods&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;The Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA) regulates laboratory testing and requires clinical labs to be certified by their state as well as the Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) before they can accept human samples for diagnostic testing.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Multiple types of CLIA certificates may be obtained based on the complexity of testing a lab conducts. CLIA levels of complexity (CLIA-waived, moderate complexity and high complexity) are addressed only as they correspond to the Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System (HCPCS) code description in the related billing and coding article.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A. &lt;strong&gt; Presumptive Testing Methods:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Presumptive UDT:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;A presumptive UDT consists of various platforms including cards, dipsticks, cassettes and cups based on qualitative competitive IA methodology with 1 or more analytes in the test. A presumptive IA test detects the presence of the amount of drug&sol;substance present in urine above a predetermined &amp;ldquo;cut-off&amp;rdquo; value and may be read by direct optical observation or by instrument assisted direct optical observation.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;A positive test result is reported when the concentration of drug is above the cutoff; a negative is reported when the concentration of drug is below the cut-off. Positive test results are presumptive but not necessarily definitive due to sensitivity and cross-reactivity limitations.&lt;sup&gt;4&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Negative test results do not necessarily indicate the absence of a drug or substance in the urine specimen.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt; The accuracy of the results of a presumptive UDT will depend on the testing environment, type of test, the drug being tested for and training of the individual conducting the test.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 12.4pt; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;This type of test should only be used when results are needed immediately.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;2&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Presumptive UDT by Instrumented Chemistry Analyzers: &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Chemistry analyzers with IA UDT technology can be used in an office or clinical laboratory setting. This test provides less immediate test results. At no time is IA technology by chemistry analyzer analysis considered confirmatory (definitive) testing.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;A presumptive positive IA test detects the presence of a drug&sol;substance in urine at or above the &amp;ldquo;cut-off&amp;rdquo; value. If the concentration of the drug is below the cut-off, the result will be negative. Presumptive positive tests are not always true positives due to sensitivity, specificity, and cross-reactivity limitations. Negative test results do not necessarily indicate the absence of a drug or substance in the urine specimen.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved&sol;cleared test platforms are available in the marketplace, as well as laboratory developed tests (LDTs) such as modified FDA approved&sol;cleared and non-FDA approved&sol;cleared platforms and&sol;or reagents. LDTs generally have been modified to test at a lower cut-off in order to detect substances that would have been missed at a higher cut-off. For example, an FDA labeled cut-off may be 300 ng&sol;mL and the LDT cut-off for the same drug may be 100 ng&sol;mL.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Presumptive UDT can be carried out at any validated cut-off concentration. Lowering of the cut-off concentration provides more stringent cutoffs for illicit drugs. LDTs may include non-FDA cleared tests not available in CLIA-waived or moderate complexity tests (e.g., tramadol, tapentadol, carisoprodol, fentanyl, zolpidem). Lowering the cutoff increases the possibility of detecting a drug when the test has been modified from the recipe of the manufacturer.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;3&quot;&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 12.45pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations of Presumptive UDT:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;Presumptive UDT testing is limited due to:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Primarily screens for drug classes rather than specific drugs, and therefore, the practitioner may not be able to determine if a different drug within the same class is causing the positive result;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Produces erroneous results due to cross-reactivity with other compounds or does not detect all drugs within a drug class;&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Given that not all prescription medications or synthetic&sol;analog drugs are detectable and&sol;or have assays available, it is unclear as to whether other drugs are present when some tests are reported as positive;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cut-off may be too high to detect presence of a drug.&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;These limitations may mean that presumptive testing is insufficient for certain clinical needs.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;An IA involves an antibody that reacts best with the stimulating drug and reacts to a lesser extent (cross-reactive) or not at all with other drugs in the drug class. While presumptive tests vary in their ability to detect illicit drugs such as tetrahydrocannabinol (THC), cocaine, 3,4-methylenedioxy-N- methylamphetamine (MDMA; &amp;ldquo;ecstasy&amp;rdquo;), and phencyclidine (PCP), they may not be optimal tests for many prescription drugs, such as: opiates, barbiturates, benzodiazepines and opioids.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;For example, opiate reagents are formulated from morphine. Consequently, the cross-reactivity for other opioids and opiates varies based on the manufacturer and lot number. The semisynthetic opioids, hydromorphone and hydrocodone, may contribute to a positive presumptive result, while the semisynthetic opioids, oxycodone and oxymorphone, will not typically be detected even at a 300 ng&sol;mL cutoff. Synthetic opioids, such as fentanyl, meperidine and methadone, will not be detected by current opiate IA testing. Consequently, a positive opiate result by IA normally necessitates more specific identification of the substance(s) that account for the positive result, and a negative result does not rule out the presence of opiates or opioids.&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Presumptive UDT reagents for benzodiazepine are typically formulated for oxazepam, a metabolite of diazepam (Valium&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;) and chlordiazepoxide (Librium&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;), the main benzodiazepines prescribed 20 years ago. However, many of the more than 10 benzodiazepines that are currently available do not cross-react with IA benzodiazepine reagents. In particular, clonazepam and lorazepam give false negative results with presumptive IA tests and may necessitate more specific identification to account for the negative result. Similarly, a positive screening test result may require definitive UDT to identify the specific drug(s).&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Synthetic&sol;analog or &amp;ldquo;designer&amp;rdquo; drugs manufactured to elude law enforcement require definitive testing for detection. Most commercially available IA reagents fail to detect designer drugs, such as psychedelic phenethylamines even at very high concentrations.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In summary, presumptive IA UDT is often unable to identify specific drugs within many drug classes, particularly within the amphetamine, barbiturate, benzodiazepine, tricyclic antidepressants, and opiate&sol;opioid drug classes. Drugs such as buprenorphine, amphetamines, benzodiazepines, and cocaine&sol;heroin yield false negative IA results due to low cross-reactivity or non-reactivity. Drugs such as fentanyl, carisoprodol, tramadol, tapentadol and synthetic designer drugs cannot be detected by presumptive IA; therefore, it may be medically necessary for clinicians to utilize definitive UDT when the presumptive tests for these drugs are negative.&lt;sup&gt;1,5&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;B. &lt;strong&gt;Definitive UDT:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;GC-MS and LC-MS&sol;MS are complex technologies that use the separation capabilities of gaseous or liquid chromatography with the analytical capabilities of mass spectrometry. These methodologies require the competency of on-site highly trained experts in this technology and interpretation of results. While these tests require different sample preparation and analytical runs, they identify specific drugs, metabolites, and most illicit substances and report the results as absent or present typically in concentrations of ng&sol;mL.&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Quantification should not be used to determine adherence with a specific dosage or time of dose of a pain medication or illicit drug for clinical purposes. Rather, the use of quantitative drug data may be important for many reasons such as in a differential patient assessment.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; For example, when several opioids are present in the urine of a patient prescribed a single opioid, quantification may help the clinician decide whether the presence of the other opioids is consistent with metabolism of the prescribed opioid, opioid contamination during manufacturing, or if more than 1 drug within a class is being used.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Quantification may also provide information in the setting of illicit drug use. Serial creatinine-corrected quantitative values may assist in the differential assessment of ongoing drug use or cessation of drug use with continued drug excretion.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GC-MS&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;GC-MS can only be performed on molecules that are volatile. If the test drug is not volatile in its own right, it must be modified or derivatized to a volatile form. To derivatize, the test drug must be extracted from the urine, eluted from the extraction device, concentrated, and then reacted with a chemical reagent to make a volatile product. Each drug class may require a different derivatizing agent. For patients on multiple classes of medications, laboratories using GC procedures must make different volatile derivatives in order to perform comprehensive testing. Since a GC column may not be able to separate more than 1 class of compounds, multiple chromatographic runs on different column types may be required to monitor multiple drug classes.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Newer GC-MS instruments often use tandem systems. GC-MS methodology allows for the testing of multiple substances but differs in ease of run.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;2&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;LC-MS&sol;MS&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;LC-MS&sol;MS is roughly 100 times more sensitive and selective, involves fewer human steps, provides quicker turn-around time, uses less specimen volume and can test for a larger number of substances simultaneously when compared to GC-MS.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; After sample preparation, it is injected into the LC-MS&sol;MS. The sample has to undergo hydrolysis to break the glucuronide bond that frees the drug and drug metabolites. Hydrolysis is followed by multiple additional steps including protein precipitation, centrifugation and purification. Deuterium-labeled isotopic internal standards are added to quantify the drugs and drug metabolites.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;The sample is injected when the mobile phase is flowing through the chromatographic column. Each drug and drug metabolite interacts with the mobile phase and stationary phase differently and moves at different speeds depending on their chemical properties. In other words, each analyte elutes at different times. Specific drugs and metabolites are identified by their retention time and quantified against isotopic internal standards for each drug and metabolite. Each drug peak has a minimum of 2 mass transmissions to be compared to drug standards (calibrators) to ensure identification.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CLIA-Certified Laboratories-Informational Only&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CLIA specifies quality standards for proficiency testing, facility administration, general laboratory systems, pre-analytic, analytic and post-analytic systems, onsite supervision requirements, personnel qualifications and responsibilities, quality control, and quality assessment.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;High complexity laboratories must ensure that testing is carried out by onsite qualified, trained personnel using validated reliable methods compliant with regulatory procedures (42 CFR Part 493). Both GC-MS and LC-MS&sol;MS require a quality program to monitor the quality and audit the competency of the staff. LC-MS&sol;MS instrument maintenance must be performed daily as well as the validation of instrument performance prior to patient specimens. Final review and approval of GC-MS and LC-MS&sol;MS results must be performed by a qualified clinical laboratory scientist as defined in 42 CFR Part 493.1489 (Testing Personnel Qualifications). A GC-MS or LC-MS&sol;MS laboratory must have a qualified laboratory director, qualified physician, or qualified clinical laboratory scientist, as provided in 42 CFR 493.1443 (Laboratory Director Qualifications).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Purpose of UDT:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Presumptive UDT may be ordered by the clinician caring for a beneficiary when it is necessary to rapidly obtain and&sol;or integrate results into clinical assessment and treatment decisions.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Definitive UDT is considered reasonable and necessary when the clinical information supplied supports the definitive testing as in:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Identifies a specific substance or metabolite that is inadequately detected by a presumptive UDT screen; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Definitively identifies specific drugs in a large family of drugs;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Identifies a specific substance or metabolite that is not detected by presumptive UDT such as fentanyl, meperidine, synthetic cannabinoids and other synthetic&sol;analog drugs;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Identifies drugs when a definitive concentration of a drug is needed to guide management (e.g., discontinuation of THC use according to a treatment plan);&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Identifies a negative or confirms a positive presumptive UDT result that is inconsistent with a patient&amp;rsquo;s self-report, presentation, medical history, or current prescribed pain medication plan;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rules out an error as the cause of a presumptive UDT result;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Identifies non-prescribed medication or illicit use for ongoing safe prescribing of controlled substances; and&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For use in a differential assessment of medication efficacy, side effects, or drug-drug interactions.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Definitive UDT may be reasonable and necessary based on patient specific indications, including historical use, medication response, and clinical assessment, when accurate results are necessary to make clinical decisions.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; &lt;strong&gt;To establish that a test is reasonable and necessary, the clinician&amp;rsquo;s rationale for the definitive UDT and the tests ordered must be documented in the patient&amp;rsquo;s medical record.&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Drug Testing Panels&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: upper-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Presumptive UDT Panels&lt;&sol;strong&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Presumptive UDT typically involves testing for multiple analytes based on the specific beneficiary&#39;s clinical history and risk assessment. This information must be documented in the medical record. Presumptive UDT may be ordered as a panel and billed as a &amp;ldquo;Per Patient encounter&amp;rdquo; regardless of the number of analytes tested.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Definitive UDT Panels&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Physician-directed definitive profile testing is reasonable and necessary when ordered for a particular patient &lt;strong&gt;based upon historical use, clinical findings and community trends&lt;&sol;strong&gt;. However, the same physician-defined profile is not reasonable and necessary for every patient in a physician&amp;rsquo;s practice. &lt;strong&gt;Definitive UDT orders should be individualized, based on clinical history and risk assessment. This information must be documented in the medical record.&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Specimen Type&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Urine or oral fluid is the preferred biologic specimen for testing because of the ease of collection, storage, and cost-effectiveness.&lt;sup&gt;1 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;UDT cannot detect the dosage of drug ingested&sol;used, the time of use, or the means of delivery (intravenous vs. oral vs. inhaled). Detection time of a substance in urine is typically 1-3 days depending on the drug, rate of metabolism, and rate of excretion. Lipid-soluble drugs, such as marijuana, may remain in body fat and be detected upwards of a week or more.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ethanol is not discussed in this LCD&lt;&sol;strong&gt;.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Ethanol is a known drug of abuse but is routinely tested in blood, not urine. In addition, the &lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;Drug Enforcement Administration&lt;&sol;span&gt; (DEA) Resource Guide&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;&sol;sup&gt; states that alcohol is exempt from control by the Controlled Substances Act (CSA).&amp;nbsp;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;text-decoration: underline;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Covered Indications for UDT&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Group A &amp;ndash; Symptomatic patients, Multiple drug ingestion and&sol;or Patients with unreliable history&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;A patient who presents in a variety of medical settings with signs or symptoms of substance use toxicity will be treated presumptively to stabilize the patient while awaiting presumptive, then definitive testing to determine the cause(s) of the presentation. The need for definitive UDT is based upon presumptive test findings, responses to medical interventions, and treatment plan.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; A presumptive UDT should be performed as part of the evaluation and management of a patient who presents in an emergency room or urgent care setting with any 1 of the following:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Coma;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Altered mental status in the absence of a clinically defined toxic syndrome or toxidrome;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Severe or unexplained cardiovascular instability (cardiotoxicity);&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Unexplained metabolic or respiratory acidosis in the absence of a clinically defined toxic syndrome or toxidrome;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Seizures with an undetermined history; or &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;To provide antagonist to specific drug(s).&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;The presumptive findings, definitive drug tests ordered, and reasons for the testing must be documented in the patient&#39;s medical record.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Group B - Diagnosis and treatment for substance abuse or dependence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;A patient in active treatment for a SUD or monitoring across different phases of recovery may undergo medical management for a variety of medical conditions. A physician who is writing prescriptions for medications to treat either the SUD or other conditions may need to know if the patient is taking substances which can interact with prescribed medications or if the patient is taking prescribed medications as expected.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; The risk of drug-drug interactions is inherent to the patient and may be compounded by prescribed medications. UDT is a medically necessary and useful component of chemical dependency diagnosis and treatment. The UDT result influences treatment and level of care decisions.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Ordered tests and testing methods (presumptive and&sol;or definitive) must match the stage of screening, treatment, or recovery; the documented history; and Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-V) diagnosis. For patients with no known indicators of risk for SUDs, the clinician may screen for a broad range of commonly abused drugs using presumptive UDT. For patients with known indicators of risk for SUDs, the clinician may screen for a broad range of commonly abused drugs using definitive UDT. For patients with a diagnosed SUD, the clinician should perform random UDT at random intervals to properly monitor the patient.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Testing profiles must be determined by the clinician based on the following medical necessity guidance criteria:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Patient history, physical examination, and previous laboratory findings; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Stage of treatment or recovery;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Suspected abused substance;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Substances that may present high risk for additive or synergistic interactions with prescribed medication (e.g., benzodiazepines, alcohol).&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;The patient&amp;rsquo;s medical record &lt;strong&gt;must include an appropriate number of UDTs billed over time based on the stage of screening, treatment, or recovery;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;sup&gt;9&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;sup&gt; &lt;&sol;sup&gt; the rationale for the drugs&sol;drug classes ordered; and the results must be documented in the medical record and used to direct care.&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Maximum Number of Allowed Presumptive UDTs for SUD&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;The number of UDTs billed over time must meet medical necessity and be documented in the patient&amp;rsquo;s medical record.&lt;sup&gt;8 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For patients with &lt;strong&gt;0 to 30 consecutive days of abstinence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, presumptive UDT is not to exceed 3 presumptive UDTs in a rolling 7 days. More than 3 presumptive UDTs in a rolling 7 days is not reasonable and necessary and is not covered by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For patients with &lt;strong&gt;31 to 90 consecutive days of abstinence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, presumptive UDT is not to exceed 3 presumptive UDTs in a rolling 7 days. More than 3 presumptive UDTs in a rolling 7 days is not reasonable and necessary and is not covered by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For patients with &lt;strong&gt;&amp;gt; 90 consecutive days of abstinence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, presumptive UDT is not to exceed 3 presumptive UDTs in a rolling 30 days. More than 3 presumptive UDTs in a rolling 30 days is not reasonable and necessary and is not covered by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Maximum Number of Allowed Definitive UDTs for SUD&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Depending on the patient&amp;rsquo;s specific substance use history, definitive UDT to accurately determine the specific drugs in the patient&amp;rsquo;s system may be necessary. Definitive testing may be ordered when accurate and reliable results are necessary to integrate treatment decisions and clinical assessment. The number of UDTs billed over time and the rationale for definitive UDT must be documented in the patient&amp;rsquo;s medical record.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For patients with &lt;strong&gt;0 to 30 consecutive days of abstinence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, definitive UDT is not to exceed 1 definitive UDT in a rolling 7 days. More than 1 definitive UDT in a rolling 7 days is not reasonable and necessary and is not covered by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For patients with &lt;strong&gt;31 to 90 consecutive days of abstinence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, definitive UDT is not to exceed 3 definitive UDTs in a rolling 30 days. More than 3 definitive UDTs in a rolling 30 days is not reasonable and necessary and is not covered by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;For patients with &lt;strong&gt;&amp;gt; 90 days of consecutive abstinence&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, definitive UDT is not to exceed 3 definitive UDTs in a rolling 90 days. More than 3 definitive UDTs in a rolling 90 days is not reasonable and necessary and is not covered by Medicare.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Group C - Treatment for patients on chronic opioid therapy (COT)&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 40px;&quot;&gt;A physician who is writing prescriptions for medications to treat chronic pain can manage a patient better if the physician knows whether the patient is consuming another medication or substance, which could suggest the possibility of SUD or lead to drug-drug interactions. Additionally, UDT may help the physician monitor for medication adherence, diversion, efficacy, side effects, and patient safety in general.&lt;sup&gt;10&lt;&sol;sup&gt; A broad cross section of the general population will develop either cancer pain syndrome or non-cancer pain which will require prolonged or chronic opioid therapy for management with normal risk of addiction inherent to the substance(s) exposed.&lt;sup&gt;11&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. COT UDT Testing Objectives:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identifies absence of prescribed medication and potential for abuse, misuse, and diversion;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identifies undisclosed substances, unsanctioned prescription medication, or illicit substances;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identifies substances that contribute to adverse events or drug-drug interactions;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provides objectivity to the treatment plan;&lt;sup&gt;10&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reinforces therapeutic compliance with the patient;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provides additional documentation demonstrating compliance with patient evaluation and monitoring;&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provides diagnostic information to help assess individual patient response to medications (e.g., metabolism, side effects, drug-drug interaction, etc.) over time for ongoing management of prescribed medications.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Medical Necessity Guidance:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;Criteria to establish medical necessity for UDT must be based on patient-specific elements identified during the clinical assessment, documented by the clinician in the patient&amp;rsquo;s medical record and minimally include the following elements&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient history, physical examination and previous laboratory findings;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Current treatment plan;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prescribed medication(s); and&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Risk assessment plan&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;National pain organizations, physician societies, and the Federation of State Medical Boards&lt;sup&gt;14 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;recommend a practical management approach to definitive UDT for COT. The number of UDTs billed over time beyond the baseline presumptive UDT must be based on individual patient needs substantiated by documentation in the patient&amp;rsquo;s medical record.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 123%; padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;Recommendations for the ordering of presumptive and definitive UDT for patients on COT are as follows:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 120px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;a. COT Baseline Testing:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 123%;&quot;&gt;Depending on the patient&amp;rsquo;s specific circumstances, initial presumptive and&sol;or definitive COT patient testing may include amphetamine&sol;methamphetamine, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, cocaine, methadone, oxycodone, tricyclic antidepressants, tetrahydrocannabinol, opioids, opiates, heroin, and synthetic&sol;analog or &amp;ldquo;designer&amp;rdquo; drugs.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 120px;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;b. COT Monitoring Testing:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ongoing testing may be medically reasonable and necessary based on the patient history, clinical assessment, including medication side effects or inefficacy, suspicious behaviors, self-escalation of dose, doctor-shopping, indications&sol;symptoms of illegal drug use, evidence of diversion, or other clinician documented change in affect or behavioral pattern.&lt;sup&gt;15&lt;&sol;sup&gt; As part of the clinical evaluation of the patient, the provider should inquire about prescription compliance and potential issues of abuse or diversion such as lost prescriptions, early refill requests, or requests for escalating dose of medication.&lt;sup&gt;15 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;The number of UDTs billed over time must be based on the individual&amp;rsquo;s risk potential.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Appropriate number of UDTs billed over time based on risk is listed in the table below.&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The clinician should perform random UDT at random intervals to properly monitor a patient.&lt;sup&gt;16 &lt;&sol;sup&gt; UDT does not have to be associated with an office visit.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patients with specific symptoms of medication aberrant behavior or misuse may be tested in accordance with this document&amp;rsquo;s guidance for monitoring patient adherence and compliance during active treatment (&amp;lt;90 days) for substance use or dependence.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;UDT Frequency Based on Validated Risk Assessment and Stratification*:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;Testing must be based on clinician&amp;rsquo;s documented medical necessity and reviewed by the clinician in the management of prescribing&sol;renewing a controlled substance for every risk group outlined below.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;table style=&quot;border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;&quot; border=&quot;4&quot; width=&quot;95%&quot; cellspacing=&quot;3&quot; cellpadding=&quot;4&quot;&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Risk Group&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Baseline&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Frequency of Testing&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Low Risk&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prior to &lt;br &sol;&gt;Initiation of &lt;br &sol;&gt;COT&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Presumptive and definitive UDT not to exceed 2 times each in a rolling 365 days for prescribed medications, non-prescribed medications that may pose a safety risk if taken with prescribed medications, and illicit substances based on patient history, clinical presentation, and&sol;or community usage.&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Moderate Risk&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prior to &lt;br &sol;&gt;Initiation of &lt;br &sol;&gt;COT&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Presumptive and definitive UDT not to exceed 2 times each in a rolling 180 days for prescription medications, non-prescribed medication that may pose a safety risk if taken with prescribed medications, and illicit substances, based on patient history, clinical presentation, and&sol;or community usage.&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt; High Risk&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prior to Initiation of COT&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Presumptive and definitive UDT not to exceed 3 times each in a rolling 90 days for prescribed medications, non-prescribed medications that may pose a safety risk if mixed with prescribed and illicit substances based on patient history, clinical presentation and&sol;or community usage.&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;&sol;tbody&gt;
&lt;&sol;table&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;*Note: Any additional definitive UDT beyond recommendations above must be justified by the clinician in the medical situations in which changes in prescribed medications may be needed, such as:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;list-style-type: none;&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient response to prescribed medication suddenly changes;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient side effect profile changes;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To assess for possible drug-drug interactions;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Change in patient&amp;rsquo;s medical condition or behavior;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patient admits to use of illicit or non-prescribed controlled substance.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Opioid Risk Tool:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The patient&amp;rsquo;s risk category must be clearly defined in the medical record and is essential in determining the number of UDTs billed over time and medical necessity.&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This tool is used as a suggestion for defining Risk and this document is just an example. Other tools accepted by the Opioid Use treating community may be used [example, accepted by Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA)].&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Opioid Risk Tool (ORT)&lt;sup&gt;17&lt;&sol;sup&gt; is a brief, self-report screening tool designed for use with adult patients in primary care settings to assess risk for opioid abuse among individuals prescribed opioids for treatment of chronic pain.&lt;sup&gt;14,15 &lt;&sol;sup&gt; Patients categorized as high-risk are at increased likelihood of future abusive drug-related behavior.&lt;sup&gt;13 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;The ORT can be administered and scored in less than 1 minute and has been validated in both male and female patients, but not in non-pain populations. This tool should be administered to patients upon an initial visit prior to beginning opioid therapy for pain management. A score of 3 or lower indicates low risk for future opioid abuse, a score of 4 to 7 indicates moderate risk for opioid abuse, and a score of 8 or higher indicates a high risk for opioid abuse.&lt;sup&gt;14,17&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;table style=&quot;border-collapse: collapse; width: 99.9986%; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;&quot; border=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
