&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mastectomy for gynecomastia&lt;br &sol;&gt;Gynecomastia is the excessive growth of the male mammary glands. These conditions can cause significant clinical manifestations when the excessive breast weight adversely affects the supporting structures of the shoulders, neck, and trunk.&lt;sup&gt;32&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mastectomy with nipple preservation or reduction mammoplasty is considered reconstructive and a covered service for males with gynecomastia Grade III and IV or abnormal breast development with redundancy.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Persists more than 3 to 4 months after the pathological causes are ruled out (e.g. not limited to testosterone deficiency, testicular tumor, liver disease, or drug induced).&lt;sup&gt;32&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Persists after 3 to 4 months of unsuccessful medical treatment for pathological gynecomastia.&lt;sup&gt;16&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pain or tenderness directly related to the breast tissue which has a clinically significant impact upon activities of daily living.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clinical symptoms refractory to a trial of analgesics or anti-inflammatory agents.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For significant clinical manifestations when the excessive breast weight adversely affects the supporting structures of the shoulders, neck and trunk.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;American Society of Plastic Surgeons&amp;rsquo; gynecomastia scale:&lt;sup&gt;16&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Grade I: Small breast enlargement with localized button of tissue that is concentrated around the areola.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Grade II: Moderate breast enlargement exceeding areola boundaries with edges that are indistinct from the chest.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Grade III: Moderate breast enlargement exceeding areola boundaries with edges that are indistinct from the chest with skin redundancy present.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Grade IV: Marked breast enlargement with skin redundancy and feminization of the breast&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Gigantomastia of Pregnancy&lt;br &sol;&gt;Medicare considers subtotal mastectomy or reduction mammoplasty for the unusual condition of Gigantomastia of Pregnancy accompanied by any of the following complications (and delivery is not imminent) medically reasonable and necessary when signs or symptoms are refractory to medical treatment or physical interventions have not adequately alleviated symptoms such as:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Massive infection&lt;sup&gt;29&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Significant hemorrhage&lt;sup&gt;29&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tissue necrosis with slough&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ulceration of breast tissue&lt;sup&gt;29&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intertriginous maceration or infection of the inframammary skin refractory to dermatologic measures.&lt;sup&gt;29&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tattooing &lt;br &sol;&gt;To correct color defects of the skin may be considered reconstructive when performed in connection with a payable post-mastectomy reconstruction, or for reconstruction following trauma or removal of cancer from an eyelid, eyebrow or lip(s).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Punch graft hair transplant may be considered reconstructive when it is performed for eyebrow(s) or symmetric hairline replacement following a burn injury, trauma or tumor removal.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Nasal surgery is defined as any procedure performed on the external or internal structures of the nose, septum or turbinate. It generally involves rearrangement or excision of the supporting bony and cartilaginous structures and incision or excision of the overlying skin of the nose.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Nasal surgery, including rhinoplasty, may be reconstructive or cosmetic in nature. Current CPT Codes do not allow distinction of cosmetic or reconstructive procedures by specific codes; therefore, categorization of each procedure is to be distinguished by the presence or absence of specific signs or symptoms.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rhinoplasty is a procedure that changes the shape or appearance of the nose while improving or preserving the nasal airway. The primary purpose for Rhinoplasty can be functional, aesthetic, or both and may include other procedures on the paranasal sinuses, septum, or turbinates.&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Septoplasty is a procedure used to correct deformities of the nasal septum which can often cause issues with airflow and difficulty breathing.&lt;sup&gt;23&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a) Rhinoplasty is considered medically reasonable and necessary when the procedure is performed for correction and repair of any of the following indications:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Secondary to trauma, disease, congenital defect with nasal airway obstruction that has not resolved after previous septoplasty&sol;turbinectomy or would not be expected to resolve with septoplasty&sol;turbinectomy alone.&lt;sup&gt;19&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;chronic, non-septal, nasal obstruction due to vestibular stenosis (i.e., collapsed internal valves).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;nasal deformity secondary to a cleft lip&sol;palate or other congenital craniofacial deformity causing a functional impairment. (e.g., cleft lip nasal deformities, choanal atresia, oronasal or oromaxillary fistula)&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b.) Septoplasty is considered medically necessary when performed for any of the following indications:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;septal deviation&sol;deformity causing nasal airway obstruction that has proved unresponsive to a trial of conservative medical management lasting at least 6 weeks (e.g. topical nasal corticosteroids, decongestants, antibiotic, allergy evaluation and therapy, etc.).&lt;sup&gt;22, 23&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;recurrent sinusitis (4 or more episodes in a year) secondary to a deviated septum that does not resolve after appropriate medical and antibiotic therapy., &lt;sup&gt;20, 23&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;recurrent epistaxis (4 or more significant episodes) related to a septal deformity.&lt;sup&gt;20&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;asymptomatic septal deformity that prevents access to other trans nasal areas when such access is required to perform medically necessary procedures (e.g., ethmoidectomy).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;performed in association with cleft lip or cleft palate repair.&lt;sup&gt;21&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;obstructed nasal breathing due to septal deformity or deviation that has proved unresponsive to medical management and is interfering with the effective use of medically necessary Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) for the treatment of an obstructive sleep disorder.&lt;sup&gt;14&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Chemical Peel&lt;br &sol;&gt;Is covered for the treatment of Actinic Keratosis.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dermabrasion is considered medical reasonable and necessary when correcting defects resulting from traumatic injury, surgery or disease.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dermabrasion, segmental, face in conjunction with antimicrobial therapy is covered for the treatment of rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by skin thickening, which can cause an enlargement of the nose due to excess tissue and overgrowth of sebaceous glands.&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Rhinophyma in its most severe cases can affect breathing and even vision.&lt;sup&gt;3,7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dermal injections for facial lipodystrophy syndrome (LDS) using dermal fillers approved by the FDA for this purpose, and then only in human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)-infected Medicare beneficiaries who manifest depression secondary to the physical stigma of HIV treatment will be covered. Effective for claims with dates of service on and after March 23, 2010.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;See Pub. 100-03, &lt;em&gt;Medicare National Coverage Determinations&lt;&sol;em&gt; Chapter 1, Coverage Determinations Part 4, Section 250.5 Dermal Injection for the Treatment of Facial Lipodystrophy Syndrome.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;See Pub. 100-04, &lt;em&gt;Claims Processing Manual&lt;&sol;em&gt;, Chapter 32, Section 260, Dermal Injection for the Treatment of Facial Lipodystrophy Syndrome.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Abdominal Lipectomy&sol;Panniculectomy&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The medical records document that the panniculus causes chronic intertrigo [dermatitis occurring on opposed surfaces of the skin, skin irritation, infection or chafing that consistently recurs or remains refractory to appropriate medical therapy (e.g., topical antifungals, corticosteroids, antibiotics)] over a period of 3 months.&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Difficulty walking or functional impairment in activities of daily living due to pannus size, chronic pain, and&sol;or ulceration created by the abdominal skin fold. Preoperative photographs may be required to support justification and should be supplied upon request.&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;This procedure may also be medically necessary for the patient that has had a significant weight-loss following the treatment of morbid obesity, in addition to meeting the criteria noted above, there should be evidence that the individual has maintained a stable weight for at least 6 months. If the weight loss is the result of bariatric surgery, abdominoplasty&sol;panniculectomy should not be performed until at least 18 months after bariatric surgery and only when weight has been stable for at least the most recent 6 months and infection and inflammation has continued for the most recent 3 months.&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The following procedures are non-covered for the following indications.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cosmetic surgery performed to treat psychiatric or emotional problems is not covered.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If a non-covered cosmetic surgery is performed in the same operative period as a covered surgical procedure, benefits will be provided for the covered surgical procedure only.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dermabrasion&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Post-acne scarring&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rosacea other than rhinophyma&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All other indications not identified as covered in the section above&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Abdominal Lipectomy&sol;Panniculectomy&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Repairing abdominal wall laxity, or diastasis recti&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Redundancies resulting from weight loss or weight loss surgery when that tissue is without evidence of chronic infection or inflammation that is refractory to conservative treatment as outlines in the indications listed above.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Solely to improve appearance&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All other indications unless covered in the section above&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;&sol;strong&gt; Abdominal Lipectomy&sol;Panniculectomy is considered experimental and investigational for minimizing the risk of hernia formation or recurrence. There is no evidence that pannus contributes to hernia formation. The primary cause of hernia formation is an abdominal wall defect or weakness, not a pulling effect from a large or redundant pannus.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Liposuction used for body contouring, weight reduction or the harvest of fat tissue for transfer to another body region for alteration of appearance or self-image or physical appearance is considered cosmetic and not covered as medically necessary.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reconstructive Breast Surgery: Removal of Breast Implants for re-implantation of an implant inserted for cosmetic purposes only and not for history of mastectomy for treatment of breast cancer, lumpectomy, or treatment of contralateral breast to bring it into symmetry with a reconstructed breast following cancer surgery is not a covered Medicare benefit.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reduction Mammaplasty
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Surgery performed primarily to reshape the breasts to improve appearance or self-image.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mammapexy unrelated to breast reconstruction following a medically necessary mastectomy.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mastectomy for gynecomastia&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Breast reduction or surgical mastectomy for gynecomastia, either unilateral or bilateral, as the first line treatment.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When performed solely to improve appearance of the male breast or to alter contours of the chest wall.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Gigantomastia of Pregnancy&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Surgery to reshape the breasts to improve appearance or self-image.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All other indications not identified as covered in the section above.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Corrective facial surgery will be considered cosmetic rather than reconstructive when there is no functional impairment present. However, some congenital, acquired, traumatic or developmental anomalies may not result in functional impairment, but are so severely disfiguring as to merit consideration for corrective surgery. These situations will be handled through the appeal process.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thyroid chrondoplasty to alter the appearance of the thyroid cartilage which is without functional defect is considered cosmetic.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rhinoplasty is not covered when performed for &lt;strong&gt;either&lt;&sol;strong&gt; of the following indications because it is considered cosmetic in nature or not medically necessary:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Solely for the purpose of changing appearance or improving self-image in the absence of any signs or symptoms of functional abnormalities.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;As a primary treatment for an obstructive sleep disorder when the above criteria for approval have not been met.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rhytidectomy is generally considered a cosmetic procedure. It may be considered medically necessary upon review to correct a functional impairment as a result of a disease state ie; facial paralysis. Often this procedure is performed in conjunction with other procedures to correct the impairment.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Social Security Act (Title XVIII)&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect; 1862(a)(1)(A) states that no Medicare payment shall be made for items or services which are not reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or injury.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Title XVIII of the SSA, &amp;sect;1833(e), prohibits Medicare payment for any claim which lacks the necessary information to process the claim.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect; 1862(a)(7). This section excludes routine physical examinations.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CFR, Title 42, Ch. IV, &amp;sect; 410.32, Diagnostic X-Rays, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions. This section describes regulations that apply to performing these tests.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;IOM Citations:&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pub 100-02, &lt;em&gt;Medicare Benefit Policy Manual&lt;&sol;em&gt;, Chapter 15 Covered Medical and Other Health Services:&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Section 80-Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, Section 280.2 -Colorectal Cancer Screening&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pub.100-04 &lt;em&gt;Medicare Claims Processing Manual&lt;&sol;em&gt; -Chapter 18 Preventive and Screening Services: Section 60 - Colorectal Cancer Screening&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CR 10901 Local Coverage Determinations (LCDs) Implementation date January 8, 2019.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;This LCD only applies to &lt;strong&gt; diagnostic&lt;&sol;strong&gt; colonoscopies and sigmoidoscopies. Refer to the Medicare Internet Only Manuals (IOM) for coverage of colorectal cancer screening procedures. &lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Sigmoidoscopy and colonoscopy testing allows for the direct visualization of the lower gastrointestinal tract. Inspection is performed with an illuminated tube. These procedures are performed to detect polyps, tumors and other lesions of the intestines. The site of pathology can be identified during a colonoscopy and a biopsy can be obtained. &lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Definitions:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sigmoidoscopy is the examination of the entire rectum and sigmoid colon, and includes examination of a portion of the descending colon.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Colonoscopy is the examination of the entire colon, from the rectum to the cecum, and may include the examination of the terminal ileum.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;strong&gt; Indications and Limitations of Coverage and&sol;or Medical Necessity&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A. The following are Medicare-covered indications for diagnostic colonoscopy:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation of an abnormality on barium enema or other imaging study, which is likely to be clinically significant, such as filling a defect or stricture.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation of unexplained gastrointestinal bleeding:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hematochezia not thought to be from rectum or perianal source,&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Melena of unknown origin; after an upper GI source has been excluded,&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Presence of fecal occult blood,&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Positive stool DNA test results. (e.g. guaiac&sol;Fecal immunochemical test {FIT &lt;br &sol;&gt;Test}&sol;Cologuard).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unexplained iron deficiency anemia.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Examination to evaluate entire colon for synchronous cancer or polyps in a patient with treatable cancer or polyp.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chronic inflammatory bowel disease of the colon if more precise diagnosis or determination of the extent of activity of disease will influence immediate management.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clinically significant diarrhea of unexplained origin with additional symptoms (e.g., with weight loss).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intraoperative identification of the site of a lesion that cannot be detected by palpation or gross inspection at surgery (e.g., polypectomy site or location of a bleeding source).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treatment of bleeding from such lesions as vascular malformation, ulceration, neoplasm, and polypectomy site (e.g., electrocoagulation, heater probe, laser or injection therapy).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Removal of foreign body.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Excision of colonic polyps.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decompression of acute nontoxic megacolon or sigmoid volvulus, pseudo obstruction of the colon (Ogilvie&amp;rsquo;s syndrome).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Balloon dilatation of stenotic lesions (e.g., anastomotic strictures).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Palliative treatment of stenosing or bleeding neoplasm.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Marking a neoplasm for localization.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation of a patient with endocarditis due to streptococcus bovis or any bacterium of enteric origin.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Suspected disease of terminal ileum.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation of acute colonic ischemia&sol;ischemic bowel disease.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In patients with Crohn&amp;rsquo;s colitis and chronic ulcerative colitis: colonoscopy every 1 or 2 years with multiple biopsies for detection of cancer and dysplasia in patients with:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pancolitis of 8 or more years duration; or&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Left-sided colitis of 15 or more years duration.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation within 6 months of the removal of sessile polyps to determine and document total excision. If evaluation indicates that residual polyp is present, excision should be done with repeat colonoscopy within 6 months. After evidence of total excision without return of the polyp, repeat colonoscopy yearly.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Patients undergoing curative resection for colon or rectal cancer should undergo a colonoscopy 1 year after the resection (or 1 year following the performance of the colonoscopy that was performed to clear the colon of synchronous disease).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;B. A diagnostic colonoscopy is not considered medically necessary for the following conditions:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chronic, stable, irritable bowel syndrome or chronic abdominal pain. There are unusual exceptions in which colonoscopy may be done to rule out organic disease, especially if symptoms are unresponsive to therapy.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Acute limited diarrhea.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hemorrhoids.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Metastatic adenocarcinoma of unknown primary site in the absence of colonic symptoms when it will not influence management.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Routine follow-up of inflammatory bowel disease (except for cancer surveillance in Crohn&#39;s colitis, chronic ulcerative colitis).&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Routine examination of the colon in patients about to undergo elective abdominal surgery for non-colonic disease.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Upper GI bleeding or melena with a demonstrated upper GI source.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;C. A diagnostic flexible sigmoidoscopy is covered for the following indications:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation of suspected distal colonic disease when there is no indication for a colonoscopy.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluation for anastomotic recurrence in rectosigmoid carcinoma.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All of the covered indications listed for a diagnostic colonoscopy.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;D. A diagnostic flexible sigmoidoscopy is not indicated when a colonoscopy is indicated.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A). Allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect; 1833(e). This section prohibits Medicare payment for any claim which lacks the necessary information to process the claim.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Change Request 10901 Local Coverage Determinations (LCDs) Implementation date January 8, 2019.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;IOM 100-08 &lt;em&gt;Medicare Program Integrity Manual&lt;&sol;em&gt;, Chapter 13-Local Coverage Determinations&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;Benefits are not available for endoluminal treatment for Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) using the Stretta&amp;reg; procedure, the Bard EndoCinch&amp;trade; Suturing System, Plicator&amp;trade;, or similar treatments as these procedures are not considered reasonable and necessary for the diagnosis or treatment of an injury or disease.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Currently, these procedures are considered non-covered due to the fact that current peer-reviewed literature does not support the efficacy of the services. Claims will be denied as &quot;not proven effective.&quot;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;The Stretta&amp;reg; procedure is an endoluminal treatment for GERD in which radiofrequency energy is delivered to smooth muscle of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). A flexible catheter equipped with special needle electrodes for precise energy delivery is placed by mouth into the esophagus and carefully controlled radiofrequency energy is then delivered to the LES and gastric cardia, creating thermal lesions. The manufacturer maintains that the changes that occur immediately, and over time, result in a &quot;tighter&quot; LES and a less compliant gastric cardia. Additionally, the interruption of nerve pathways in the LES area is believed to reduce the incidence of inappropriate LES &quot;relaxations,&quot; leading to an improvement in GERD symptoms. Substantial peer-reviewed evidence to fully support these assumptions remains to be published.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;The Bard EndoCinch&amp;trade; Suturing System and the Plicator&amp;trade; are intended for use in endoscopic placement of suture(s) in the soft tissue of the esophagus and stomach and for approximation of tissue for treatment of symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;Clinical data from various studies are emerging. At this time, open-label studies or patient registries with short term follow-ups are the dominant source of data. The overwhelming preponderance of reviewers remains equivocal in their support and have called for randomized controlled trials with long-term follow-ups. In the absence of evidence from such studies, and in the absence of wide acceptance, endoscopic treatments for GERD are not proven effective.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Therefore, they are not reimbursable even though some of the treatments may have associated CPT&amp;trade; or OPPS codes.&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Coverage for the TIF (Transoral Incisionless Fundoplication) procedure is for treatment of patients in whom proton pump inhibitor therapy fails. An example of the device used in TIF is EsophyX&amp;trade;. TIF using EsophyX&amp;trade; for performing surgery for treating gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) reconstructs the valve at the top of the stomach that helps prevents acid reflux.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Indications&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Coverage is appropriate for TIF if done by a well-trained surgeon for the following indications:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;1. Symptomatic chronic gastroesophageal reflux (chronic being defined as &amp;gt; 6 months of symptoms), and&lt;br &sol;&gt;&amp;nbsp;2. Symptoms must not be completely responsive to Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) as judged by GERD HRQL scores of &amp;lt; or equal to 12 while on PPIs and &amp;gt; &amp;nbsp; or equal to 20 when off for 14 days (also acceptable would be the difference of &amp;gt; or equal to 10 of the scores between off and on therapy), and&lt;br &sol;&gt;&amp;nbsp;3. Hiatal hernia &amp;lt; or equal to 2 cm, including where the hernia has been reduced to 2 cm or less by a successful laparoscopic hernia reduction procedure prior to the &lt;strong&gt;TIF&lt;&sol;strong&gt; procedure. (Based on (FDA) approval).&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Coverage is not extended:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 30px;&quot;&gt;1. For those patients who may have recurrent symptoms or may fail this procedure. No literature has been submitted for repeat TIF use. These procedures (repeat TIF) would be considered investigational at this time.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;42 CFR &amp;sect;410.32(a) Diagnostic x-ray tests, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;80 Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, &amp;sect;80.1.1 Certification Changes&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This is a limited coverage policy for molecular biomarker tests to guide targeted therapy selection in Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA).&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Coverage criteria:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient is an adult with a confirmed diagnosis of moderately to severely active RA.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;2&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient has a history of failure, contraindication, or intolerance to at least one first-line therapy for the treatment of RA (i.e., conventional synthetic disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drugs (csDMARDs)) despite adequate dosing.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;3&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient has not initiated a biologic or targeted synthetic therapy (b&sol;tDMARD) for RA (i.e., Tumor Necrosis Factor-alpha&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;inhibitor [TNFi], Janus Kinase [JAK] inhibitor, etc.) OR has initiated b&sol;tDMARD therapy and is being considered for an alternate class of targeted therapies as a result of failure, contraindication, or intolerance to the initial targeted therapy despite adequate dosing.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;4&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test predicts response and&sol;or non-response to at least one class of targeted or biologic therapies for RA according to multiple validated response&sol;remission criteria (a) with an accuracy that exceeds that which can be obtained from the combination of existing clinical and other data AND (b) with demonstrated reproducibility across clinical study cohorts.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;5&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Testing using molecular biomarkers has not been previously performed for predictive therapy selection in RA. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;6&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Testing is performed according to the intended use of the test in the intended patient population for which the test was developed and validated.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;7&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test demonstrates analytical validity (AV), clinical validity (CV) and clinical utility (CU), establishing a clear and significant biological&sol;molecular basis for stratifying patients and subsequently selecting (either positively or negatively) a clinical management in a clearly defined population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;8&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Clinical validity of any analytes (or expression profiles) measured must be established through a study published in the peer-reviewed published literature for the intended use of the test in the intended population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;9&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;If the test relies on an algorithm, the algorithm must be validated in a cohort that is not a development cohort for the algorithm.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;10&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The lab providing the test is responsible for clearly indicating to treating physicians the population and indication(s) for test use.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;11&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test successfully completes a Molecular Diagnostic Services Program (MolDX&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;) Technical Assessment that ensures that AV, CV, and CU criteria set in this policy are met to establish the test as Reasonable and Necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;12&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; If applicable, performance characteristics are equivalent or &lt;&sol;span&gt;superior to the average performance of other similar tests (for the same intended use) evaluated by this contractor upon successful completion of a technical assessment.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Since the clinical utility of predictive testing is largely dependent upon consensus-based management recommendations, this coverage decision is subject to change pending changes in the literature and in consensus guidelines. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%; background: white;&quot;&gt;Finally, new tests that become available with significantly improved performance may render older tests no longer compliant with this policy.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;42 CFR &amp;sect;410.32(a) Diagnostic x-ray tests, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;80 Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, &amp;sect;80.1.1 Certification Changes&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This contractor will provide limited coverage for molecular tests to aid in the diagnosis or exclusion of lung cancer in a patient with an indeterminate pulmonary nodule (IPN) following a non-diagnostic bronchoscopy when ALL of the following conditions are met:&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;1&quot; type=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary has undergone bronchoscopy for an indeterminate pulmonary nodule AND &lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;1&quot; type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The bronchoscopy has failed to provide a specific histopathological diagnosis such that further diagnostic procedures would otherwise be considered necessary to pursue a specific diagnosis (non-diagnostic bronchoscopy); AND &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Test results will be used to meaningfully inform patient management within the framework of nationally recognized consensus guidelines.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The nodule cannot or will not be evaluated by an alternate methodology (EBUS, FNA, etc.) for a specific diagnosis prior to receipt of molecular test results.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary does NOT have any of the following:&lt;&sol;span&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;1&quot; type=&quot;a&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Personal history of lung cancer &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Current diagnosis of cancer or high clinical suspicion for cancer&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;An overall low risk for pulmonary malignancy such that test results would not meaningfully alter patient management and significantly improve patient outcomes.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;An overall high risk for pulmonary malignancy such that test results would not meaningfully alter patient management and significantly improve patient outcomes.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary has not been tested with the same or similar assay for the same clinical indication.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The beneficiary is within the population and has the indication for which the test was developed and is covered. The lab providing the test is responsible for clearly indicating to treating clinicians the population and indication for test use.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test has demonstrated clinical validity and utility, establishing a clear and significant biological&sol;molecular basis for stratifying patients and subsequently selecting (either positively or negatively) a clinical management decision in a clearly defined population. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Clinical validity of any analytes (or expression profiles) measured must be established through a study published in the peer-reviewed literature for the intended use of the test in the intended population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Rule-out tests should have a high sensitivity and negative predictive value (NPV) such that patients can be safely selected for a less aggressive management strategy without delay to diagnosis due to false negative results.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Rule-in tests should have a high specificity and positive predictive value (PPV) such that patients can be safely selected for more aggressive management without significantly increasing procedures in patients without cancer due to false positive results.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test demonstrates analytical validity including both analytical and clinical validations. If the test relies on an algorithm (which may range in complexity from a threshold determination of a single numeric value to a complex mathematical or computational function), the algorithm must be validated in a cohort that is not a development cohort for the algorithm.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Tests utilizing a similar methodology or evaluating a similar molecular analyte to a test for which there is a generally accepted testing standard or for which existing coverage exists must demonstrate equivalent or superior test performance (i.e., sensitivity and&sol;or specificity) when used for the same indication in the same intended-use population. New tests that become available with significantly improved performance may render older tests no longer compliant with this policy.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The test successfully completes a Molecular Diagnostic Services Program (MolDX&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;) technical assessment that ensures the test is reasonable and necessary as described above.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;NOTE: Next Generation Sequencing (NGS) performed to identify genetic variants in samples classified as malignant is not within the scope of this policy but may fall under other established policies. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

cms_cov_policy:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Title XVIII of the Social Security Act, &amp;sect;1862(a)(1)(A) allows coverage and payment for only those services that are considered to be reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;42 CFR &amp;sect;410.32(a) Diagnostic x-ray tests, diagnostic laboratory tests, and other diagnostic tests: Conditions&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;CMS Internet-Only Manual, Pub. 100-02, Medicare Policy Manual, Chapter 15, &amp;sect;80 Requirements for Diagnostic X-Ray, Diagnostic Laboratory, and Other Diagnostic Tests, &amp;sect;80.1.1 Certification Changes&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;This is a coverage policy for gene expression profile tests that assess risk or predict therapeutic response in men who have an established diagnosis of castration resistant or metastatic prostate cancer. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Such testing is considered reasonable and necessary to help guide treatment decisions in men with prostate cancer and a life expectancy such that they are candidates for prostate cancer treatment according to the most recent nationally recognized guidelines at the time of testing or based on FDA labelling of drugs and biologics available as potential treatment options.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The scope of this policy includes gene expression profile tests regardless of methodology. It is exclusive of targeted and comprehensive genomic profiles (CGP) by next generation sequencing (NGS) and single biomarker expression analyses. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Coverage criteria:&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1. BOTH of the following criteria must be met:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The beneficiary is being actively managed for castration resistant or metastatic (hormone sensitive or castration resistant) prostate cancer.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The beneficiary is within the population and has the indication for which the test was developed and validated.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2. At least 1 of the following criteria are met:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient is a candidate for more than one management option, which could be considered to have varied, or increasing&sol;decreasing levels of intensity based on a nationally recognized consensus guideline, and the physician and patient must decide among these treatments OR&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The patient is a candidate for more than one management option, and the test has shown that it predicts response to a specific therapy among accepted therapy options based on nationally recognized consensus guidelines and&sol;or FDA labelling.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;3. The patient has not been tested with the same or similar test for the same intended use.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;4. The patient has not received pelvic radiation or androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) prior to the biopsy or prostate resection specimen on which the test will be performed. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The only exception to this is for men who are na&amp;iuml;ve to secondary systemic therapies (that could be given after ADT monotherapy) AND at least 1 of the following is true:&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;padding-left: 80px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;a. They do not have other standard-of-care drug-targetable gene alterations to guide systemic therapy, as defined in nationally recognized guidelines OR&lt;br &sol;&gt;b. They have other standard-of-care drug-targetable gene alterations to guide systemic therapy, as defined in nationally recognized guidelines but they are not eligible for those therapies for another reason.&lt;br &sol;&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;5&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; The test demonstrates analytical validity (AV), clinical validity (CV) and clinical utility (CU), establishing a clear and significant biological&sol; molecular basis for stratifying patients and subsequently selecting (either positively or negatively) a clinical management in a clearly defined population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;6&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Clinical validity of any analytes (or expression profiles) measured must be established through a study published in the peer-reviewed literature for the intended use of the test in the intended population.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;7&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; If the test relies on an algorithm, the algorithm must be validated in a cohort that is not a development cohort for the algorithm.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;8&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; Testing must be performed according to Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA) and&sol;or Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulations in an accredited laboratory.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;9&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; The lab providing the test is responsible for clearly indicating to treating physicians the population and indication(s) for test use.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol start=&quot;10&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; The test successfully completes a Molecular Diagnostic Services Program (MolDX&lt;sup&gt;&amp;reg;&lt;&sol;sup&gt;) Technical Assessment that ensures that AV, CV, and CU criteria set in this policy are met to establish the test as Reasonable and Necessary.&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Genomic expression profile tests that demonstrate equivalent or superior analytical and clinical validity to those covered by this contractor will be considered reasonable and necessary for the same indications. &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;

