indication:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Chronic venous disorders of the lower extremity incorporate a spectrum of morphologic and functional abnormalities of the venous system. Symptoms and findings include pain, leg heaviness, aching, swelling, skin dryness, tightness, itching, irritation, and muscle cramps. Clinical signs of venous disease include dilated &lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;veins (e.g. telangiectasia, varicose veins), leg edema, skin changes (lipodermatosclerosis; a fibrosing dermatitis of the subcutaneous tissue), and skin ulceration. The presence of symptoms and clinical signs of venous disease correlates with the presence of venous reflux (&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;in the superficial or deep veins) and&sol;or venous obstruction&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt; &lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background: white;&quot;&gt;identified on duplex ultrasound.&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
