&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Spongy Layer&lt;&sol;strong&gt;, loosely connected to the chorionic membrane, is highly concentrated with proteoglycans and glycoproteins including hyaluronic acid, as well as type I, III, and IV collagen.&lt;sup&gt;5,6,8,9&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;middle Fibroblast layer&lt;&sol;strong&gt; is made up of type I, III, V, and VI collagen.&lt;sup&gt;6,8,9&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Compact layer&lt;&sol;strong&gt; that sits adjacent to the basement membrane is composed of collagen Types I, III, V, and VI, along with fibronectin.&lt;sup&gt;5,9&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;basement membrane&lt;&sol;strong&gt; anchors the epithelial layer&lt;sup&gt;14 &lt;&sol;sup&gt;and contains collagen Types IV, V and VII, fibronectin, laminin, and hyaluronic acid.&lt;sup&gt;6,10&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Adjacent to the basement membrane and in immediate contact with the amniotic fluid is the single layer of epithelial cells. &lt;strong&gt;Amniotic epithelial cells&lt;&sol;strong&gt; produce type III and IV collagen, glycoproteins such as laminin and fibronectin, which in turn form become the basement membrane.&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;The amniotic membrane&amp;rsquo;s purpose is to house and physically protect the fetus, but additional functions include regulation of the pH of the amniotic fluid, transportation of water and soluble material between the mother and fetus, and the synthesis of numerous growth factors and cytokines. The amniotic membrane also secretes anti-inflammatory proteins. All of this results in these tissues having anti-inflammatory, anti-microbial, anti-fibroblastic, and non-immunogenic properties.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Amniotic products have been asserted to be a source of stem cells. Both the amniotic epithelial layer (fetal derived cells) and mesenchymal (avascular stromal) layer derived from the embryonic mesoderm contain their respective stem cells that can differentiate into multiple cell lines, including myocytes, osteocytes, and chondrocytes.&lt;sup&gt;8,9&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Amniotic fluid also is found to contain amniotic mesenchymal stem cells.&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;The chorionic membrane (adjacent to the mother&amp;rsquo;s endometrium during development of the fetus), umbilical cord, Wharton&amp;rsquo;s jelly, and umbilical cord blood have also been found to contain mesenchymal stem cells.&lt;sup&gt;7,11&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Under normal conditions, placental tissues are collected via aseptic technique during cesarean section. Protocols vary as to how the tissues are harvested, prepared, preserved, and stored. Testing is also required to ensure these tissues do not carry any communicable diseases transmissible from donor to recipient.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: normal;&quot;&gt;Because the spongy layer loosely connects the amniotic membrane to the chorionic membrane, these two layers are easily separated upon blunt dissection at initial harvesting.&lt;sup&gt;10&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Other than ease of separation between amniotic and chorionic membranes, the following steps in processing the tissues into the desired form vary, based on the portions of the placental tissues extracted, sterilization processes (if any) undertaken, and method of preservation utilized. Common methods of preservation include cryopreservation, lyophilization (freeze-drying), glycerol-preservation, &amp;gamma; (gamma)-sterilization, low heat dehydration, and vitrification. &lt;sup&gt;5,8,9,10&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;A process called &amp;ldquo;Decellularization&amp;rdquo; using physical, biological, chemical or combination of methods may be used to remove all cellular and nuclear materials while preserving the extracellular matrix components.&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Removal of all cellular components is thought to lessen the possibility of eliciting an immune response.&lt;sup&gt;3,10&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Different decellularization processes are available. Eventual preparation of sheets, particulate suspensions, liquids, or gels allows the final marketed product.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.1pt;&quot;&gt;Depending on the methods utilized, the processing of placental and amniotic-based tissues will affect the viability of cellular components, growth factors, and other valuable properties for which these tissues are promoted. To date, there are significant differences that exist in the processing of different placental and amniotic-based tissue products. &lt;sup&gt;3,5&lt;&sol;sup&gt; Lack of consistency and standardization within propriety manufacturing (preparation) processes precludes determination and comparison of the final product form, characteristics, properties, and components. Placental-Based Products are not analogous to stem cells as many placental-based products are void of stem cell or living cells after processing.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.05pt;&quot;&gt;The Food and Drug Administration (FDA), under Sect. 361 of the Public Health Service Act (regulated by the Centers for Biologics Evaluation and Research CBER, an arm of the FDA) oversees the therapeutic use of &amp;ldquo;Human cells or tissue products&amp;rdquo; or &amp;ldquo;HCT&sol;P&amp;rdquo;s. Once these types of products are harvested, their processing and handling will determine whether the products fall under Section 361 guidance or default to the more regulated section 351 of the Public Health Service Act and&sol;or the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act. The regulatory pathway for pre-market FDA approval of new drugs, devices and&sol;or biological products, requires registration as a New Drug application (NDA), a Premarket Approval (PMA) or other appropriate device premarket clearance such as 510(k), or a (BLA) Biologics License Approval.&lt;sup&gt;8,9,13,14&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;If a human cells or tissue product meets Section 361 FDA requirements, the product will not require FDA pre-market review and approval. To meet &amp;ldquo;Section 361&amp;rdquo; FDA regulatory requirements, the placental&sol;amniotic-based tissue product must meet the following 4 criteria:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;The HCT&sol;P is:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Minimally Manipulated&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intended for Homologous Use (as reflected by the labeling, advertising, or other indications of the manufacturer&amp;rsquo;s objective intent)&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The manufacture of the HCT&sol;P does not involve the combination of the cells or tissues with another agent, except for water, crystalloids, or a sterilizing, preserving, or storage agent, provided that the addition of water, crystalloids, or the sterilizing, preserving, or storage agent does not raise new clinical safety concerns with respect to the HCT&sol;P&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Either:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-roman;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The HCT&sol;P does not have a systemic effect and is not dependent upon the metabolic activity of living cells for its primary function; or&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The HCT&sol;P has a systemic effect or is dependent upon the metabolic activity of living cells for its primary function, and:
&lt;ol style=&quot;list-style-type: lower-alpha;&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is for autologous use.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is for allogeneic use in a first-degree or second-degree blood relative.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is for reproductive use.&lt;sup&gt;13&lt;&sol;sup&gt;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ol&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;Due to the ongoing development of new products and clinical trials, the field of FDA regulatory requirements is evolving. It is the expectation that the respective Medicare Administrative Contractor will continue to follow any guidance as it is issued by the FDA.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;Lack of standard formulation, dose, frequency of administration, and standard of care in treatment with these products further complicates regulation and guidance determinations.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;Despite this lack of standardization, numerous amniotic and placental-derived products have been released for use in treatment of musculoskeletal conditions. These conditions include, but are not limited to tendon&sol;ligament injuries, musculoskeletal injuries, cartilage damage, osteoarthritis, or pain related of these conditions as well as an adjunctive orthopedic surgical treatments. Due to the lack of component standardization, the remainder of this LCD will use the term amniotic and placental-derived products to mean ANY product derived from ANY combination of amniotic membrane&sol;chorion&sol;placenta&sol;Wharton&amp;rsquo;s jelly&sol;umbilical cord&sol;&sol;amniotic fluid&sol;umbilical cord blood.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 14.15pt;&quot;&gt;Although amniotic and placental-derived products are marketed to treat certain musculoskeletal conditions, there is limited available support for safety and efficacy from human clinical trials.&lt;&sol;p&gt;

